{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba Docs/AI-Pipeline/00_MASTER_CONTEXT.md | sed -n '1,220p'; nl -ba Docs/AI-Pipeline/00_MASTER_CONTEXT.md | sed -n '221,460p'; nl -ba Docs/AI-Pipeline/00_MASTER_CONTEXT.md | sed -n '461,680p'; nl -ba Docs/AI-Pipeline/01_MILESTONE_TASK_GRAPH.md | sed -n '1,390p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# No Safe Circle — Autonomous AI Development Pipeline\r\n     2\t\r\n     3\t## Master Context / Architecture Handoff\r\n     4\t\r\n     5\tUse this file for the durable target architecture when continuing work on the autonomous development pipeline.\r\n     6\t\r\n     7\tFor exact current implementation status, always read `CURRENT_STATE.md` first.\r\n     8\t\r\n     9\tLast architecture/status reconciliation: 2026-08-21, after Milestone 1 completion and the post-M1 adversarial-architecture-review branch merge.\r\n    10\t\r\n    11\t## Current Architecture Status\r\n    12\t\r\n    13\tThe architecture is no longer purely prospective.\r\n    14\t\r\n    15\t**Milestone 1 is implemented and merged.** The repository now contains:\r\n    16\t\r\n    17\t- a human-approved persistent work graph under `Tasks/`;\r\n    18\t- stable `NSC-###` work IDs;\r\n    19\t- reconciliation-key provenance;\r\n    20\t- deterministic graph validation;\r\n    21\t- deterministic dependency/parent hierarchy reconstruction;\r\n    22\t- exclusive-resource metadata;\r\n    23\t- project-level non-code requirements;\r\n    24\t- a working local `taskcontrol` CLI;\r\n    25\t- a real deterministic ready frontier.\r\n    26\t\r\n    27\tThe current graph contains 37 work records and the first real `taskcontrol ready` calculation returned seven executable one-agent implementation tasks.\r\n    28\t\r\n    29\tThe next planned infrastructure milestone is Milestone 2: targeted GDD/project context, Progressive Decomposition, and Artifact Authority.\r\n    30\t\r\n    31\tBefore substantial Milestone 2 expansion, the M1-complete architecture should be reviewed through `Pipeline/ArchitectureReview/` and any accepted architectural corrections should be persisted in `DECISIONS.md` and `CURRENT_STATE.md`.\r\n    32\t\r\n    33\t## Project\r\n    34\t\r\n    35\tRepository: `https://github.com/cathode26/NoSafeCircle`\r\n    36\t\r\n    37\tCourse work already provides reusable building blocks:\r\n    38\t\r\n    39\t- Assignment 3: Agent Crew concept — Planner → Implementer → Validator.\r\n    40\t- Assignment 4: RAG/retrieval over the No Safe Circle GDD.\r\n    41\t- Assignment 5: goal-oriented gap analysis and bounded implementation agent.\r\n    42\t- Assignment 6: working GER implementation — Implement/Generate → Evaluate → Refine → Circuit Breaker, including runtime-feedback repair.\r\n    43\t- Assignment 7: not yet implemented; intended to become a scored Style Evaluator specialization when real authorized style-sensitive artifact work exists.\r\n    44\t\r\n    45\tThe production goal is to evolve these pieces into a system that can continue developing No Safe Circle while keeping project state durable, observable, resumable after model/context failures, bounded in cost, grounded in real Unity evidence, and protected from silent AI invention of missing game design.\r\n    46\t\r\n    47\t## Core Architectural Decision\r\n    48\t\r\n    49\tThe LLM does **not** own durable project state or the continuous autonomous loop.\r\n    50\t\r\n    51\tA local deterministic supervisor eventually owns the loop.\r\n    52\t\r\n    53\tClaude or another coding model is a bounded worker that receives one small piece of approved work at a time.\r\n    54\t\r\n    55\tPersistent project state lives in repository artifacts and later GitHub operational state, not in a model context window.\r\n    56\t\r\n    57\tA worker may implement or generate work, but it does not declare that work complete. Completion is determined by project evidence plus merge/approval state.\r\n    58\t\r\n    59\tAn implementation worker also does not silently invent missing game design. Missing design becomes an explicit artifact proposal.\r\n    60\t\r\n    61\t## Desired End-to-End Loop\r\n    62\t\r\n    63\t```text\r\n    64\tGDD / Root Canon\r\n    65\t ↓\r\n    66\tPersistent Work Graph                         ← M1 COMPLETE\r\n    67\t ↓\r\n    68\tReady / Near-Ready Frontier                   ← M1 COMPLETE\r\n    69\t ↓\r\n    70\tTargeted Task + GDD + Project Context          ← M2 NEXT\r\n    71\t ↓\r\n    72\tProgressive Decomposer\r\n    73\t ↓\r\n    74\tEnough approved information?\r\n    75\t ├─ yes → Concrete child work\r\n    76\t └─ no  → Proposed artifact\r\n    77\t               ↓\r\n    78\t         Artifact Authority Gate\r\n    79\t               ↓\r\n    80\t          Authorized?\r\n    81\t          /       \\\r\n    82\t        yes        no\r\n    83\t         ↓          ↓\r\n    84\t   Artifact Generator   Re-plan / Human Review\r\n    85\t         ↓\r\n    86\t      Artifact GER\r\n    87\t         ├─ Canon / Design Evaluator\r\n    88\t         ├─ Completeness Evaluator\r\n    89\t         └─ Style Evaluator when applicable\r\n    90\t         ↓\r\n    91\t   Approved Artifact\r\n    92\t         ↓\r\n    93\t   Back to Decomposer\r\n    94\t         ↓\r\n    95\t   Concrete Work Item\r\n    96\t         ↓\r\n    97\tLocal Supervisor                               ← M3\r\n    98\t ↓\r\n    99\tClaim Ticket\r\n   100\t ↓\r\n   101\tGit Branch + Git Worktree + Draft PR\r\n   102\t ↓\r\n   103\tBuild Small Context Package\r\n   104\t ↓\r\n   105\tBounded Worker / Agent Crew\r\n   106\t ↓\r\n   107\t==============================\r\n   108\t        GER EXECUTION LOOP                     ← M4 production integration\r\n   109\t------------------------------\r\n   110\tImplement / Generate\r\n   111\t ↓\r\n   112\tTask/GDD/Artifact Evaluation\r\n   113\t ↓\r\n   114\tDeterministic Validation\r\n   115\t ↓\r\n   116\tUnity / Runtime Validation\r\n   117\t ↓\r\n   118\tPASS?\r\n   119\t ├─ yes → Approved Work\r\n   120\t └─ no  → Feedback Bundle → Refiner → Re-evaluate\r\n   121\t                         ↓\r\n   122\t                 retry/cost/runtime limit\r\n   123\t                         ↓\r\n   124\t                  Circuit Breaker\r\n   125\t==============================\r\n   126\t ↓\r\n   127\tFresh AI Diff Review\r\n   128\t ↓\r\n   129\tPR Merge / Artifact Approval\r\n   130\t ↓\r\n   131\tWork = Complete\r\n   132\t ↓\r\n   133\tDependency Graph Recalculates\r\n   134\t ↓\r\n   135\tNew Work Becomes Ready\r\n   136\t ↓\r\n   137\tContinuous Safe Backlog Processing             ← M5\r\n   138\t```\r\n   139\t\r\n   140\tThe exact evaluator order may vary by work type, but cheap deterministic failures should be detected before spending model tokens on semantic review whenever practical.\r\n   141\t\r\n   142\t## Guiding Principle\r\n   143\t\r\n   144\t**Use deterministic code for facts and computation. Use LLMs for semantic judgment and bounded implementation.**\r\n   145\t\r\n   146\tDeterministic/local responsibilities include:\r\n   147\t\r\n   148\t- work identity and dependency bookkeeping;\r\n   149\t- graph validation/readiness;\r\n   150\t- file enumeration;\r\n   151\t- code/component detection;\r\n   152\t- Unity YAML inspection;\r\n   153\t- `.meta` GUID relationships;\r\n   154\t- obvious project-state queries;\r\n   155\t- Git/scope checks;\r\n   156\t- compilation/test orchestration;\r\n   157\t- progress/no-op detection;\r\n   158\t- budget/circuit-breaker bookkeeping.\r\n   159\t\r\n   160\tLLM responsibilities include:\r\n   161\t\r\n   162\t- semantic decomposition of bounded near-frontier work;\r\n   163\t- deciding whether approved information is sufficient to decompose;\r\n   164\t- distinguishing missing design from oversized implementation scope;\r\n   165\t- interpreting genuinely ambiguous intent;\r\n   166\t- bounded implementation planning/coding;\r\n   167\t- semantic task/GDD evaluation;\r\n   168\t- fresh semantic diff review;\r\n   169\t- authorized design/content generation;\r\n   170\t- style evaluation/refinement;\r\n   171\t- repair from structured failure evidence.\r\n   172\t\r\n   173\t## Sources of Truth\r\n   174\t\r\n   175\t### Root Canon\r\n   176\t\r\n   177\tThe current GDD is root game-design canon.\r\n   178\t\r\n   179\tRAG should retrieve only relevant canonical chunks instead of repeatedly sending the entire GDD to a model.\r\n   180\t\r\n   181\tPrior assignment artifacts may provide history or reusable machinery, but they do not override current GDD or integrated repository state.\r\n   182\t\r\n   183\t### Approved Design Extensions\r\n   184\t\r\n   185\tApproved artifact outputs may later extend root canon with project-authorized detail.\r\n   186\t\r\n   187\tAn artifact becomes trusted only after this path:\r\n   188\t\r\n   189\t```text\r\n   190\tArtifact proposal\r\n   191\t ↓\r\n   192\tArtifact Authority Gate\r\n   193\t ↓\r\n   194\tAuthorized generation\r\n   195\t ↓\r\n   196\tRequired evaluators / GER\r\n   197\t ↓\r\n   198\tApproved artifact\r\n   199\t ↓\r\n   200\tTrusted downstream input\r\n   201\t```\r\n   202\t\r\n   203\tApproved artifacts may add detail only inside the authority granted to them. They may not contradict or silently replace GDD canon.\r\n   204\t\r\n   205\tUnapproved drafts are not trusted design input.\r\n   206\t\r\n   207\t### Codebase Truth\r\n   208\t\r\n   209\tThe current integrated `main` branch is the authority for what implementation actually exists and is complete.\r\n   210\t\r\n   211\tOld assignment planning output is not current codebase truth unless reconciled against the repository.\r\n   212\t\r\n   213\t### Durable Work Definition\r\n   214\t\r\n   215\tThe living operational work graph now exists under:\r\n   216\t\r\n   217\t```text\r\n   218\tTasks/\r\n   219\t  NSC-001.yaml\r\n   220\t  ...\r\n   221\t  NSC-037.yaml\r\n   222\t```\r\n   223\t\r\n   224\tInitial supported work kinds are:\r\n   225\t\r\n   226\t```text\r\n   227\tfeature\r\n   228\tartifact\r\n   229\timplementation\r\n   230\t```\r\n   231\t\r\n   232\tDefinitions:\r\n   233\t\r\n   234\t- `feature` — organizational/high-level work that may require progressive decomposition; never a direct implementation handoff.\r\n   235\t- `artifact` — work whose output is approved design/content; complete only after required authority/evaluation/promotion.\r\n   236\t- `implementation` — concrete repository work that can be executed when dependencies and execution scope allow it.\r\n   237\t\r\n   238\tStable `reconciliation_key` values preserve traceability back to the reconciliation record that proposed each initial work item.\r\n   239\t\r\n   240\t### Operational Status\r\n   241\t\r\n   242\tDurable task definitions currently use `open` / `complete`.\r\n   243\t\r\n   244\tLater, GitHub Issues/Projects should provide human-facing transient execution visibility such as:\r\n   245\t\r\n   246\t- Backlog\r\n   247\t- Ready\r\n   248\t- In Progress\r\n   249\t- Blocked\r\n   250\t- Validating\r\n   251\t- Needs Review\r\n   252\t- Done\r\n   253\t\r\n   254\tDo not make ephemeral “worker is running” state a constantly committed task-file concern if the supervisor/GitHub can own it safely.\r\n   255\t\r\n   256\t## Milestone 1 Persistent Work Graph\r\n   257\t\r\n   258\tMilestone 1 is complete. See `01_MILESTONE_TASK_GRAPH.md` for its completion record.\r\n   259\t\r\n   260\tThe initial graph was created through:\r\n   261\t\r\n   262\t```text\r\n   263\tGDD + repository state\r\n   264\t ↓\r\n   265\timmutable reconciliation snapshot\r\n   266\t ↓\r\n   267\tindependent multi-model verification\r\n   268\t ↓\r\n   269\tbounded repair / re-verification\r\n   270\t ↓\r\n   271\t0 final material findings\r\n   272\t ↓\r\n   273\thuman approval\r\n   274\t ↓\r\n   275\tdeterministic bootstrap\r\n   276\t ↓\r\n   277\tpersistent graph\r\n   278\t```\r\n   279\t\r\n   280\tApproved reconciliation:\r\n   281\t\r\n   282\t`20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5`\r\n   283\t\r\n   284\tSuccessful verification:\r\n   285\t\r\n   286\t`20260821T195959Z-43dba5de`\r\n   287\t\r\n   288\tThe one-time bootstrap is marked by:\r\n   289\t\r\n   290\t`Pipeline/TaskGraph/BOOTSTRAP_PERSISTED.json`\r\n   291\t\r\n   292\tDo not rerun the initial `--apply` bootstrap after that marker exists.\r\n   293\t\r\n   294\t### Current Task-Control Interface\r\n   295\t\r\n   296\t```text\r\n   297\tpython Pipeline/TaskGraph/taskcontrol.py validate\r\n   298\tpython Pipeline/TaskGraph/taskcontrol.py list\r\n   299\tpython Pipeline/TaskGraph/taskcontrol.py show NSC-003\r\n   300\tpython Pipeline/TaskGraph/taskcontrol.py ready\r\n   301\tpython Pipeline/TaskGraph/taskcontrol.py graph\r\n   302\t```\r\n   303\t\r\n   304\tA work item is executable-ready only when it is open, is an `artifact` or `implementation`, has `execution_scope: single_agent`, and all dependencies are complete.\r\n   305\t\r\n   306\tThis readiness calculation is deterministic.\r\n   307\t\r\n   308\t## Reconciliation Boundary\r\n   309\t\r\n   310\tReconciliation is an immutable point-in-time observation, not the mutable work database.\r\n   311\t\r\n   312\tEvery full reconciliation run creates a new versioned snapshot of GDD requirements versus repository state.\r\n   313\t\r\n   314\tA later reconciliation may propose additions/changes/conflicts against the persistent graph, but the Reconciliation Agent does not directly rewrite `Tasks/*.yaml`.\r\n   315\t\r\n   316\tFuture graph changes must cross an explicit deterministic diff/review/apply boundary.\r\n   317\t\r\n   318\tOld reconciliation snapshots remain historically true even after later implementation changes the repository.\r\n   319\t\r\n   320\tThe persistent graph is the living operational state.\r\n   321\t\r\n   322\t## Progressive Task Decomposition\r\n   323\t\r\n   324\tThe graph should **not** contain the entire game's lowest-level implementation plan in advance.\r\n   325\t\r\n   326\tHigh-level work is decomposed just in time as it approaches the actionable frontier.\r\n   327\t\r\n   328\tThe Progressive Decomposer must first determine *why* an item is not a safe one-agent handoff.\r\n   329\t\r\n   330\tTwo distinct cases exist:\r\n   331\t\r\n   332\t1. **Design decomposition** — approved design/content is missing or too coarse. Identify the smallest missing artifact needed to continue.\r\n   333\t2. **Execution decomposition** — design is concrete, but the implementation record still bundles too many responsibilities/validation targets. Split only already-approved responsibilities.\r\n   334\t\r\n   335\tThe Decomposer must not generate missing design during the same decision that detects the gap.\r\n   336\t\r\n   337\tDetecting missing design and creating missing design are separate authority events.\r\n   338\t\r\n   339\t## Artifact Authority\r\n   340\t\r\n   341\tBefore a proposed design/content artifact may be generated, the Artifact Authority Gate answers:\r\n   342\t\r\n   343\t1. Is the artifact necessary to progress parent work?\r\n   344\t2. Which current GDD requirements or approved artifacts authorize it?\r\n   345\t3. Which design decisions may it make?\r\n   346\t4. Which areas must it not invent, replace, or contradict?\r\n   347\t\r\n   348\tPossible outcomes conceptually include:\r\n   349\t\r\n   350\t```text\r\n   351\tAUTHORIZED\r\n   352\tREJECTED\r\n   353\tNEEDS_HUMAN_REVIEW\r\n   354\t```\r\n   355\t\r\n   356\tThe gate authorizes existence/generation. It does not judge the quality of the generated artifact.\r\n   357\t\r\n   358\tAfter authorization, Artifact GER and applicable evaluator profiles determine whether the artifact is good enough to promote to trusted input.\r\n   359\t\r\n   360\t## Assignment 3 Integration\r\n   361\t\r\n   362\tAssignment 3's crew belongs **inside bounded ticket execution**:\r\n   363\t\r\n   364\t```text\r\n   365\tPersistent Work Graph chooses WHAT\r\n   366\t        ↓\r\n   367\tTask Context\r\n   368\t        ↓\r\n   369\tPlanner\r\n   370\t        ↓\r\n   371\tImplementer\r\n   372\t        ↓\r\n   373\tValidator\r\n   374\t```\r\n   375\t\r\n   376\tThe crew should not reconstruct the global roadmap.\r\n   377\t\r\n   378\t## Assignment 4 Integration\r\n   379\t\r\n   380\tAssignment 4 RAG answers:\r\n   381\t\r\n   382\t> What does the GDD say about this work?\r\n   383\t\r\n   384\tIt supplies canonical evidence to context building, decomposition, semantic evaluation, and refinement.\r\n   385\t\r\n   386\tRAG is desired-state/canon retrieval, not repository-state truth.\r\n   387\t\r\n   388\tMilestone 2 should reuse/wrap this machinery rather than rebuild a second RAG system without need.\r\n   389\t\r\n   390\t## Assignment 5 Integration\r\n   391\t\r\n   392\tAssignment 5's goal-oriented lesson is now represented by persistent work state plus progressive decomposition.\r\n   393\t\r\n   394\tFull gap/reconciliation analysis should not run before every feature.\r\n   395\t\r\n   396\tUse full reconciliation when justified by events such as:\r\n   397\t\r\n   398\t- material GDD change;\r\n   399\t- milestone completion;\r\n   400\t- low/empty backlog;\r\n   401\t- significant newly discovered dependency;\r\n   402\t- disagreement between repository and work graph;\r\n   403\t- explicit human request.\r\n   404\t\r\n   405\tNormal progress uses the persistent graph.\r\n   406\t\r\n   407\tAssignment 5's implementation agent may also be reused as a bounded implementer/refiner, as Assignment 6 demonstrated.\r\n   408\t\r\n   409\t## Assignment 6 / GER Integration\r\n   410\t\r\n   411\tAssignment 6 proved the central repair-loop concept needed later for production execution:\r\n   412\t\r\n   413\t```text\r\n   414\tImplement / Generate\r\n   415\t ↓\r\n   416\tEvaluate\r\n   417\t ↓\r\n   418\tValidation Evidence\r\n   419\t ├─ PASS → approved\r\n   420\t └─ FAIL → structured feedback → Refiner → re-evaluate\r\n   421\t                                      ↓\r\n   422\t                               retry to budget\r\n   423\t                                      ↓\r\n   424\t                               Circuit Breaker\r\n   425\t```\r\n   426\t\r\n   427\tThe camera run established production rules:\r\n   428\t\r\n   429\t1. Static evaluator PASS is not sufficient for interactive Unity behavior.\r\n   430\t2. Runtime observations must re-enter the repair loop as structured evidence.\r\n   431\t3. Validation history should be auditable enough to reconstruct what happened.\r\n   432\t4. A Refiner that makes no relevant change while failures remain has not made progress.\r\n   433\t5. No-op repair attempts count against the repair budget.\r\n   434\t6. Circuit Breakers bound retries and escalate instead of looping forever.\r\n   435\t7. Evaluator contracts should be task/GDD specific, not merely “does it compile?”\r\n   436\t\r\n   437\tReference:\r\n   438\t\r\n   439\t`Assignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md`\r\n   440\t\r\n   441\t## Validation Evidence Model\r\n   442\t\r\n   443\tProduction execution should build an evidence bundle rather than trust one PASS/FAIL source.\r\n   444\t\r\n   445\tPossible evidence sources include:\r\n   446\t\r\n   447\t```text\r\n   448\tGit / scope validation\r\n   449\tStatic checks\r\n   450\tUnity compilation\r\n   451\tEditMode tests\r\n   452\tPlayMode tests\r\n   453\tRuntime observations\r\n   454\tSimulation/adversarial-agent results (later)\r\n   455\tTask/GDD semantic evaluation\r\n   456\tArtifact canon/design evaluation\r\n   457\tArtifact completeness evaluation\r\n   458\tStyle evaluation when applicable\r\n   459\tFresh semantic diff review\r\n   460\t```\r\n   461\t\r\n   462\tNot every work item needs every evaluator.\r\n   463\t\r\n   464\tWork type, source requirements, acceptance criteria, and risk determine which evidence is required.\r\n   465\t\r\n   466\tFailures should be normalized into structured Refiner input.\r\n   467\t\r\n   468\t## Assignment 7 / Style Evaluator Integration\r\n   469\t\r\n   470\tAssignment 7 should not become a parallel autonomous architecture.\r\n   471\t\r\n   472\tWhen Progressive Decomposition identifies an authorized style-sensitive artifact, Assignment 7 can supply a scored evaluator inside Artifact GER:\r\n   473\t\r\n   474\t```text\r\n   475\tGenerator\r\n   476\t ↓\r\n   477\tStyle Evaluator\r\n   478\t ↓\r\n   479\tSCORE + REASON\r\n   480\t ↓\r\n   481\tRefiner\r\n   482\t ↓\r\n   483\tStyle Evaluator\r\n   484\t```\r\n   485\t\r\n   486\tThe Style Evaluator judges quality/style of content already authorized to exist.\r\n   487\t\r\n   488\tIt does not authorize creation of new canon or design content.\r\n   489\t\r\n   490\tArtifact authority and artifact quality remain separate decisions.\r\n   491\t\r\n   492\t## Git Model\r\n   493\t\r\n   494\tAutonomous implementation workers must not develop directly on `main`.\r\n   495\t\r\n   496\tFor each implementation ticket, Milestone 3 should eventually create a branch and worktree such as:\r\n   497\t\r\n   498\t```text\r\n   499\tbranch: claude/NSC-003-player-movement\r\n   500\tworktree: .worktrees/NSC-003/\r\n   501\t```\r\n   502\t\r\n   503\tExpected lifecycle:\r\n   504\t\r\n   505\t```text\r\n   506\tReady → Claimed → In Progress → Validating → PR Ready → Merged → Done\r\n   507\t```\r\n   508\t\r\n   509\t“Done” means merged/integrated evidence supports completion, not merely that a worker says it finished.\r\n   510\t\r\n   511\tDraft PRs should be opened early so humans can inspect ongoing work.\r\n   512\t\r\n   513\t## Autonomous Supervisor\r\n   514\t\r\n   515\tThe supervisor, not the model, owns the eventual continuous loop.\r\n   516\t\r\n   517\tConceptually:\r\n   518\t\r\n   519\t```python\r\n   520\twhile game_not_complete:\r\n   521\t    ready = taskcontrol.ready()\r\n   522\t\r\n   523\t    if not ready:\r\n   524\t        expand_near_frontier_or_reconcile()\r\n   525\t        continue\r\n   526\t\r\n   527\t    work = choose_safe_ready_work(ready)\r\n   528\t\r\n   529\t    if work_needs_decomposition(work):\r\n   530\t        run_progressive_decomposition(work)\r\n   531\t        continue\r\n   532\t\r\n   533\t    if work.kind == \"artifact\":\r\n   534\t        authorize_generate_evaluate_or_escalate(work)\r\n   535\t        continue\r\n   536\t\r\n   537\t    claim(work)\r\n   538\t    create_branch_worktree_and_draft_pr(work)\r\n   539\t    context = build_compact_context(work)\r\n   540\t    result = run_bounded_execution_ger(work, context)\r\n   541\t\r\n   542\t    if result.needs_human:\r\n   543\t        preserve_and_escalate(work)\r\n   544\t        continue\r\n   545\t\r\n   546\t    if validation_and_review_pass(result):\r\n   547\t        merge(work)\r\n   548\t        mark_complete(work)\r\n   549\t```\r\n   550\t\r\n   551\tThis remains target architecture; the supervisor itself is not yet implemented.\r\n   552\t\r\n   553\t## Safety / Cost Controls\r\n   554\t\r\n   555\tPer-work limits should eventually include:\r\n   556\t\r\n   557\t- maximum agent runs;\r\n   558\t- maximum repair attempts;\r\n   559\t- maximum runtime;\r\n   560\t- model/token budget;\r\n   561\t- maximum no-progress attempts.\r\n   562\t\r\n   563\tStop/escalate for:\r\n   564\t\r\n   565\t- repeated validation failure;\r\n   566\t- GDD ambiguity;\r\n   567\t- unauthorized design expansion;\r\n   568\t- architecture-changing dependencies;\r\n   569\t- Unity scene/prefab/shared-resource conflict;\r\n   570\t- scope expansion;\r\n   571\t- budget exhaustion;\r\n   572\t- repeated no-op refinement.\r\n   573\t\r\n   574\tAutonomous means “continue safe bounded work,” not “spend forever” or “invent whatever is missing.”\r\n   575\t\r\n   576\t## Parallelism\r\n   577\t\r\n   578\tDo not start with multiple coding workers.\r\n   579\t\r\n   580\tFirst make one-ticket execution reliable and restartable.\r\n   581\t\r\n   582\tLater parallelism must honor exclusive-resource claims and be conservative around Unity scenes, prefabs, ProjectSettings, Input Actions, and shared builder scripts.\r\n   583\t\r\n   584\t## Near-Term Build Strategy\r\n   585\t\r\n   586\tMilestone 1 is finished. Do not rebuild it.\r\n   587\t\r\n   588\tThe current strategy is:\r\n   589\t\r\n   590\t1. confirm `taskcontrol validate` and `taskcontrol ready` remain truthful;\r\n   591\t2. run/review the post-M1 adversarial architecture checkpoint;\r\n   592\t3. use one real ready/near-ready No Safe Circle task as the anchor for the next slice;\r\n   593\t4. build the smallest targeted task-context path needed for that work;\r\n   594\t5. reuse Assignment 4 RAG and existing deterministic reconciliation/scanner capabilities where practical;\r\n   595\t6. add Progressive Decomposition only when a near-frontier item actually needs it;\r\n   596\t7. create artifact proposals rather than invent missing design;\r\n   597\t8. authorize/evaluate generated artifacts before downstream use;\r\n   598\t9. add the single-ticket supervisor only after compact task context is reliable;\r\n   599\t10. productionize generic GER/Unity validation around real tickets;\r\n   600\t11. enable continuous autonomous backlog processing only after single-ticket execution is trustworthy;\r\n   601\t12. add parallel workers last.\r\n   602\t\r\n   603\tA strong current real-task anchor is `NSC-003` Player Movement because it is already ready, bounded, and unlocks multiple later systems.\r\n   604\t\r\n   605\tThis keeps pipeline development tied to capstone progress.\r\n   606\t\r\n   607\t## High-Level Build Milestones\r\n   608\t\r\n   609\t1. **Persistent work artifacts + deterministic `taskcontrol` — COMPLETE**\r\n   610\t2. **RAG canon service + deterministic project scanner + compact context builder + Progressive Decomposer + Artifact Authority Gate — NEXT**\r\n   611\t3. One-ticket autonomous supervisor with claims + branch/worktree/Draft PR\r\n   612\t4. Productionize GER execution + deterministic/Unity/runtime validation + evaluator profiles\r\n   613\t5. Continuous scheduling + blocker handling + budgets + GitHub visibility + eventual safe parallelism\r\n   614\t\r\n   615\tAssignment 6 has already produced a working prototype of Milestone 4's central repair-loop concept, so Milestone 4 is production integration rather than invention from zero.\r\n   616\t\r\n   617\t## Working Rule\r\n   618\t\r\n   619\tBuild in small stages.\r\n   620\t\r\n   621\tDo not try to implement the full autonomous pipeline in one context or coding session.\r\n   622\t\r\n   623\tEach meaningful session should leave durable repository state/documentation so the next session can resume without the previous chat transcript.\r\n   624\t\r\n   625\tFrom Milestone 2 onward, infrastructure should increasingly be proven by advancing actual No Safe Circle gameplay rather than becoming a separate project that indefinitely delays the game.\r\n     1\t# Context 1 — Persistent Work Artifacts + Dependency Graph\r\n     2\t\r\n     3\t## Status\r\n     4\t\r\n     5\t**COMPLETE — 2026-08-21**\r\n     6\t\r\n     7\tMilestone 1 is implemented, validated against the real No Safe Circle graph, merged into `main`, and present on `adversarial-architecture-review`.\r\n     8\t\r\n     9\tDo not treat this file as an instruction to rebuild Milestone 1. It is now the completion record and semantic reference for the persistent work graph.\r\n    10\t\r\n    11\tThe next pipeline milestone is:\r\n    12\t\r\n    13\t`Docs/AI-Pipeline/02_RAG_SCANNER_CONTEXT.md`\r\n    14\t\r\n    15\tCurrent live status is always summarized in:\r\n    16\t\r\n    17\t`Docs/AI-Pipeline/CURRENT_STATE.md`\r\n    18\t\r\n    19\t## Goal That Was Achieved\r\n    20\t\r\n    21\tMilestone 1 replaced repeated full-LLM reconstruction of project work state with a durable deterministic local graph.\r\n    22\t\r\n    23\tThe project can now remember:\r\n    24\t\r\n    25\t- what work exists;\r\n    26\t- stable work identity;\r\n    27\t- parent/feature structure;\r\n    28\t- dependencies;\r\n    29\t- execution scope;\r\n    30\t- exclusive-resource conflicts;\r\n    31\t- project-level non-code constraints;\r\n    32\t- what is complete/open;\r\n    33\t- and which concrete one-agent tasks are executable right now.\r\n    34\t\r\n    35\tNo LLM is required to load, validate, inspect, or calculate readiness for the persistent graph.\r\n    36\t\r\n    37\t## Bootstrap Provenance\r\n    38\t\r\n    39\tThe initial graph was not seeded directly from an unverified LLM plan.\r\n    40\t\r\n    41\tIt passed this trust path:\r\n    42\t\r\n    43\t```text\r\n    44\tCurrent GDD + repository state\r\n    45\t        ↓\r\n    46\tImmutable reconciliation snapshot\r\n    47\t        ↓\r\n    48\tIndependent multi-model verification\r\n    49\t        ↓\r\n    50\tBounded refinement / re-verification\r\n    51\t        ↓\r\n    52\t0 final material findings\r\n    53\t        ↓\r\n    54\tHuman approval manifest\r\n    55\t        ↓\r\n    56\tDeterministic Work Graph Seeder\r\n    57\t        ↓\r\n    58\tPersistent Tasks/*.yaml\r\n    59\t```\r\n    60\t\r\n    61\tApproved source reconciliation:\r\n    62\t\r\n    63\t`20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5`\r\n    64\t\r\n    65\tSuccessful verification:\r\n    66\t\r\n    67\t`20260821T195959Z-43dba5de`\r\n    68\t\r\n    69\tApproval manifest:\r\n    70\t\r\n    71\t`Pipeline/TaskGraph/APPROVED_BOOTSTRAP.json`\r\n    72\t\r\n    73\tBootstrap completion marker:\r\n    74\t\r\n    75\t`Pipeline/TaskGraph/BOOTSTRAP_PERSISTED.json`\r\n    76\t\r\n    77\tThe bootstrap marker is published last. Its hashes bind the initial seed state historically; they are not live immutable checksums that prevent legitimate later task-state updates.\r\n    78\t\r\n    79\t## Persistent Graph Produced\r\n    80\t\r\n    81\tThe initial approved graph contains:\r\n    82\t\r\n    83\t- 37 work records\r\n    84\t- 12 `feature`\r\n    85\t- 25 `implementation`\r\n    86\t- 0 `artifact` at initial bootstrap\r\n    87\t- 36 `open`\r\n    88\t- 1 `complete`\r\n    89\t- 59 dependency edges\r\n    90\t- 36 parent edges\r\n    91\t- 7 exclusive-resource groups\r\n    92\t- 17 project-level non-code requirements\r\n    93\t- one root: `NSC-001` / `no-safe-circle`\r\n    94\t\r\n    95\tThe only initially complete implementation is:\r\n    96\t\r\n    97\t`NSC-023 — Fixed Isometric Camera`\r\n    98\t\r\n    99\tPersistent task files:\r\n   100\t\r\n   101\t```text\r\n   102\tTasks/NSC-001.yaml\r\n   103\t...\r\n   104\tTasks/NSC-037.yaml\r\n   105\t```\r\n   106\t\r\n   107\tPersistent metadata:\r\n   108\t\r\n   109\t```text\r\n   110\tPipeline/TaskGraph/WORK_ID_MAP.json\r\n   111\tPipeline/TaskGraph/PROJECT_REQUIREMENTS.yaml\r\n   112\tPipeline/TaskGraph/RESOURCE_GROUPS.yaml\r\n   113\tPipeline/TaskGraph/BOOTSTRAP_PERSISTED.json\r\n   114\t```\r\n   115\t\r\n   116\tThe `.yaml` records use a deterministic JSON-compatible YAML 1.2 subset. This intentionally keeps the loader on Python's standard `json` parser and avoids adding a YAML dependency merely for the persistent graph.\r\n   117\t\r\n   118\t## Stable IDs and Traceability\r\n   119\t\r\n   120\tThe project root is permanently allocated:\r\n   121\t\r\n   122\t`no-safe-circle → NSC-001`\r\n   123\t\r\n   124\tThe remaining initial IDs preserve the approved seed-record order.\r\n   125\t\r\n   126\tEach persistent task keeps a `reconciliation_key` linking the operational work item back to the reconciliation record that proposed it.\r\n   127\t\r\n   128\tOnce persisted, IDs are durable work identity. Future reconciliation must not recalculate or renumber them merely because a new snapshot has different ordering.\r\n   129\t\r\n   130\t## Work Kinds\r\n   131\t\r\n   132\tMilestone 1 supports:\r\n   133\t\r\n   134\t```text\r\n   135\tfeature\r\n   136\tartifact\r\n   137\timplementation\r\n   138\t```\r\n   139\t\r\n   140\t### `feature`\r\n   141\t\r\n   142\tOrganizational or high-level work that may require later progressive decomposition.\r\n   143\t\r\n   144\tFeature nodes are not directly handed to implementation workers.\r\n   145\t\r\n   146\t### `artifact`\r\n   147\t\r\n   148\tWork whose output is an approved design/content artifact.\r\n   149\t\r\n   150\tMilestone 1 can represent this kind even though no artifact records were needed in the initial bootstrap. Artifact authority, generation, evaluation, and promotion belong to Milestone 2+.\r\n   151\t\r\n   152\t### `implementation`\r\n   153\t\r\n   154\tConcrete project work that can eventually be executed by an implementation worker when dependencies and execution scope permit it.\r\n   155\t\r\n   156\t## Execution Scope Semantics\r\n   157\t\r\n   158\tExecution scope is separate from design completeness/decomposition state.\r\n   159\t\r\n   160\tAllowed values:\r\n   161\t\r\n   162\t- `single_agent` — safe bounded one-agent handoff.\r\n   163\t- `needs_execution_decomposition` — approved design is concrete enough, but implementation responsibilities are still too broad for one safe handoff.\r\n   164\t- `human_integration_required` — the next meaningful action fundamentally requires human integration/editor judgment.\r\n   165\t- `not_applicable` — feature/organizational or already-complete work.\r\n   166\t- `unknown` — insufficiently reviewed/legacy state; not safe for autonomous selection.\r\n   167\t\r\n   168\tThe initial graph contains:\r\n   169\t\r\n   170\t- 16 `single_agent`\r\n   171\t- 8 `needs_execution_decomposition`\r\n   172\t- 13 `not_applicable`\r\n   173\t\r\n   174\tProgressive Decomposition remains a later semantic step:\r\n   175\t\r\n   176\t1. **design decomposition** when approved information is missing/too coarse;\r\n   177\t2. **execution decomposition** when design is already approved but one implementation record is too broad.\r\n   178\t\r\n   179\tExecution decomposition may split known responsibilities. It may not invent new mechanics or content.\r\n   180\t\r\n   181\t## Status Semantics\r\n   182\t\r\n   183\tMilestone 1 persistent work uses:\r\n   184\t\r\n   185\t- `open`\r\n   186\t- `complete`\r\n   187\t\r\n   188\tOperational transient states such as Claimed/In Progress/Validating belong later in the supervisor/GitHub layer rather than being committed constantly into every task branch.\r\n   189\t\r\n   190\tProduction completion semantics remain:\r\n   191\t\r\n   192\t- implementation work is complete only when integrated repository evidence supports the claim, eventually requiring merge to `main`;\r\n   193\t- artifact work is complete only after required authority/evaluation and promotion to trusted project input;\r\n   194\t- feature nodes are organizational and are not direct execution tickets.\r\n   195\t\r\n   196\t## Reconciliation Boundary\r\n   197\t\r\n   198\tReconciliation is an immutable observation snapshot, not the mutable task database.\r\n   199\t\r\n   200\tA new reconciliation run:\r\n   201\t\r\n   202\t1. observes current GDD/repository state;\r\n   203\t2. creates a new immutable snapshot;\r\n   204\t3. may propose graph additions/changes/conflicts;\r\n   205\t4. never directly rewrites the persistent graph.\r\n   206\t\r\n   207\tThe living operational state is `Tasks/*.yaml`.\r\n   208\t\r\n   209\tFuture graph changes must cross an explicit deterministic diff/review/apply boundary. Safe cascading readiness changes are computed from the graph, not written by an LLM.\r\n   210\t\r\n   211\t## Core Implementation\r\n   212\t\r\n   213\tMilestone 1 implementation lives under:\r\n   214\t\r\n   215\t`Pipeline/TaskGraph/`\r\n   216\t\r\n   217\tImportant files include:\r\n   218\t\r\n   219\t- `bootstrap_inputs.py` — loads the human-approved immutable verification artifacts and rechecks bound hashes/invariants.\r\n   220\t- `work_graph_transform.py` — deterministic stable-ID allocation and in-memory transformation.\r\n   221\t- `work_graph_validate.py` — structural, dependency, hierarchy, execution-scope, resource-group, provenance, and project-requirement validation.\r\n   222\t- `work_graph_persist.py` — staged persistence, reload/revalidation, publication, and final completion marker.\r\n   223\t- `seed_work_graph.py` — dry-run/apply entry point for the one-time initial bootstrap.\r\n   224\t- `persistent_work_graph.py` — live persisted-graph loader.\r\n   225\t- `taskcontrol.py` — deterministic graph CLI.\r\n   226\t- associated smoke tests.\r\n   227\t\r\n   228\tThe initial bootstrap is one-shot. Do not rerun `seed_work_graph.py --apply` after `BOOTSTRAP_PERSISTED.json` exists.\r\n   229\t\r\n   230\t## `taskcontrol`\r\n   231\t\r\n   232\tImplemented commands:\r\n   233\t\r\n   234\t```text\r\n   235\tpython Pipeline/TaskGraph/taskcontrol.py validate\r\n   236\tpython Pipeline/TaskGraph/taskcontrol.py list\r\n   237\tpython Pipeline/TaskGraph/taskcontrol.py show NSC-003\r\n   238\tpython Pipeline/TaskGraph/taskcontrol.py ready\r\n   239\tpython Pipeline/TaskGraph/taskcontrol.py graph\r\n   240\t```\r\n   241\t\r\n   242\t### `validate`\r\n   243\t\r\n   244\tThe live loader and graph validator check the current persistent state rather than blindly trusting that the initial seed was valid forever.\r\n   245\t\r\n   246\tValidation covers, among other things:\r\n   247\t\r\n   248\t- duplicate IDs/reconciliation keys;\r\n   249\t- filename/ID consistency;\r\n   250\t- valid ID format;\r\n   251\t- ID-map/task consistency;\r\n   252\t- valid kind/status/execution-scope enums;\r\n   253\t- malformed required fields;\r\n   254\t- one correct project root;\r\n   255\t- valid parent references;\r\n   256\t- connected/acyclic parent hierarchy;\r\n   257\t- valid dependencies;\r\n   258\t- dependency acyclicity;\r\n   259\t- bootstrap provenance consistency;\r\n   260\t- exclusive-resource group consistency;\r\n   261\t- project-requirement structure;\r\n   262\t- open executable work incorrectly marked `not_applicable`.\r\n   263\t\r\n   264\t### `ready`\r\n   265\t\r\n   266\tA work item is executable-ready when:\r\n   267\t\r\n   268\t1. `status == open`;\r\n   269\t2. `kind` is `artifact` or `implementation`;\r\n   270\t3. `execution_scope == single_agent`;\r\n   271\t4. every `depends_on` item is complete.\r\n   272\t\r\n   273\tFeature nodes, decomposition-needed work, human-integration work, and unknown-scope work are not returned as executable-ready.\r\n   274\t\r\n   275\tThe first real ready frontier contained seven tasks:\r\n   276\t\r\n   277\t```text\r\n   278\tNSC-003  Mouse-Directed Player Movement, Shared Pointer Projection, and Movement Restriction\r\n   279\tNSC-004  Player Health Ownership, Restore, Death Transition, and Feedback\r\n   280\tNSC-005  Player Mana Ownership, Restart Reset, and Denied-Cast Feedback\r\n   281\tNSC-011  Active Enemy Registry\r\n   282\tNSC-020  Shared Doorway-Crossing State (Forward-Side Crossing Detection)\r\n   283\tNSC-024  Tilemap and AI Navigation Package Configuration\r\n   284\tNSC-037  Windows Build Scene Registration\r\n   285\t```\r\n   286\t\r\n   287\tThis was the key Milestone 1 proof: the repository can now answer **what can safely execute now** without rebuilding a roadmap in an LLM context.\r\n   288\t\r\n   289\t### `list`, `show`, and `graph`\r\n   290\t\r\n   291\t`list` provides the compact full backlog with status/kind/execution scope.\r\n   292\t\r\n   293\t`show <ID>` exposes the bounded task contract including dependencies, exclusive resources, acceptance criteria, validation requirements, notes, and bootstrap repository state.\r\n   294\t\r\n   295\t`graph` reconstructs the feature hierarchy and dependency edges in human-readable text.\r\n   296\t\r\n   297\t## Real First-Task Example\r\n   298\t\r\n   299\t`NSC-003` is a concrete `single_agent` task with no dependencies and is a strong anchor for the next pipeline slice.\r\n   300\t\r\n   301\tIts contract includes:\r\n   302\t\r\n   303\t- mouse-directed click/hold movement through Unity Input System/Input Actions;\r\n   304\t- shared cursor-to-gameplay-plane projection;\r\n   305\t- owner-controlled movement restriction for Charged Fireball;\r\n   306\t- owner-controlled reset entry point;\r\n   307\t- owner-controlled gameplay suspend/re-enable behavior;\r\n   308\t- Unity integration through the relevant Input Actions, builder, and canonical scene resources.\r\n   309\t\r\n   310\tMilestone 2 should use real near-term gameplay work like this when testing compact context generation rather than building generic infrastructure in isolation.\r\n   311\t\r\n   312\t## Resource-Conflict Semantics\r\n   313\t\r\n   314\tExclusive resource claims describe integration/conflict surfaces; they do not automatically imply dependency edges.\r\n   315\t\r\n   316\tInitial shared resource groups include logical ownership surfaces plus concrete Unity assets such as:\r\n   317\t\r\n   318\t- `Assets/InputSystem_Actions.inputactions`\r\n   319\t- `Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs`\r\n   320\t- `Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scripts/DoorInteractable.cs`\r\n   321\t- `Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Scenes/DoorPrototype.unity`\r\n   322\t\r\n   323\tThese become especially important later when the supervisor introduces claims/worktrees and eventually parallel workers.\r\n   324\t\r\n   325\t## Lessons Preserved from Milestone 1\r\n   326\t\r\n   327\t1. **Repository state beats conversation memory.**\r\n   328\t2. **Reconciliation is observation, not mutable project state.**\r\n   329\t3. **Human approval is a real authority boundary.**\r\n   330\t4. **The graph should be coarse and truthful rather than exhaustively speculative.**\r\n   331\t5. **Execution size and design completeness are different questions.**\r\n   332\t6. **A resource collision is not automatically a dependency.**\r\n   333\t7. **Deterministic code owns IDs, dependency bookkeeping, validation, and readiness.**\r\n   334\t8. **LLMs should be reserved for semantic judgment/decomposition/implementation where they add value.**\r\n   335\t9. **Infrastructure must quickly feed real game implementation.**\r\n   336\t10. **Do not fully decompose distant features just to make the graph look complete.**\r\n   337\t\r\n   338\t## Completion Criteria — Satisfied\r\n   339\t\r\n   340\tMilestone 1 satisfies its intended completion criteria:\r\n   341\t\r\n   342\t- work files are parseable;\r\n   343\t- stable persistent IDs exist;\r\n   344\t- `feature`, `artifact`, and `implementation` are supported;\r\n   345\t- graph validation catches malformed/unsafe graph state;\r\n   346\t- parent and dependency graphs are connected/acyclic as required;\r\n   347\t- readiness is deterministic;\r\n   348\t- feature nodes are not returned as executable work;\r\n   349\t- `single_agent` execution scope is enforced by readiness;\r\n   350\t- the initial graph was reconciled against current GDD/repository state rather than copied from old assignment output;\r\n   351\t- seeded records preserve `reconciliation_key` traceability;\r\n   352\t- reconciliation history is immutable and cannot directly rewrite `Tasks/*.yaml`;\r\n   353\t- dependency/status changes cascade through deterministic computation;\r\n   354\t- the graph distinguishes ordinary blocking from execution-decomposition and human-integration states;\r\n   355\t- the real graph identifies a truthful executable frontier.\r\n   356\t\r\n   357\t## Next\r\n   358\t\r\n   359\tMilestone 1 is closed.\r\n   360\t\r\n   361\tContinue with:\r\n   362\t\r\n   363\t`Docs/AI-Pipeline/02_RAG_SCANNER_CONTEXT.md`\r\n   364\t\r\n   365\tBefore substantial Milestone 2 infrastructure, review the M1-complete architecture through:\r\n   366\t\r\n   367\t`Pipeline/ArchitectureReview/README.md`\r\n   368\t\r\n   369\tThen build the smallest Milestone 2 context/decomposition slice that directly advances real No Safe Circle gameplay.\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba Docs/AI-Pipeline/02_RAG_SCANNER_CONTEXT.md | sed -n '1,430p'; nl -ba Docs/AI-Pipeline/03_SUPERVISOR_GIT_GITHUB_CONTEXT.md | sed -n '1,180p'; nl -ba Docs/AI-Pipeline/04_EXECUTION_GER_VALIDATION_CONTEXT.md | sed -n '1,190p'; nl -ba Docs/AI-Pipeline/05_CONTINUOUS_AUTONOMY_CONTEXT.md | sed -n '1,170p'; nl -ba Docs/AI-Pipeline/DECISIONS.md | sed -n '1,340p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba Docs/AI-Pipeline/02_RAG_SCANNER_CONTEXT.md | sed -n '1,430p'; nl -ba Docs/AI-Pipeline/03_SUPERVISOR_GIT_GITHUB_CONTEXT.md | sed -n '1,180p'; nl -ba Docs/AI-Pipeline/04_EXECUTION_GER_VALIDATION_CONTEXT.md | sed -n '1,190p'; nl -ba Docs/AI-Pipeline/05_CONTINUOUS_AUTONOMY_CONTEXT.md | sed -n '1,170p'; nl -ba Docs/AI-Pipeline/DECISIONS.md | sed -n '1,340p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Context 2 — RAG + Project Scanner + Progressive Decomposer\n     2\t\n     3\t## Goal\n     4\t\n     5\tReduce Claude token usage by making local tools answer factual questions, retrieve only relevant GDD evidence, and let Claude progressively decompose only the bounded work that is close to execution.\n     6\t\n     7\tThis milestone also introduces the Artifact Authority Gate so missing design is never silently invented.\n     8\t\n     9\tPrerequisite: Context 1's persistent work graph and `taskcontrol` exist.\n    10\t\n    11\t## Architecture\n    12\t\n    13\t```text\n    14\tFeature / Work Item\n    15\t        │\n    16\t        ├── source requirement IDs → Assignment 4 RAG → relevant GDD chunks\n    17\t        │\n    18\t        ├── approved artifact dependencies → authorized design extensions\n    19\t        │\n    20\t        └── scope/current state → local project scanner → repository evidence\n    21\t                                      ↓\n    22\t                                 context pack\n    23\t                                      ↓\n    24\t                                   Claude\n    25\t                                      ↓\n    26\t                           Progressive Decomposer\n    27\t                                      ↓\n    28\t                       Enough information to execute?\n    29\t                              /              \\\n    30\t                            yes               no\n    31\t                             ↓                 ↓\n    32\t                   Concrete child work    Artifact Proposal\n    33\t                                               ↓\n    34\t                                      Artifact Authority Gate\n    35\t                                               ↓\n    36\t                                           authorized?\n    37\t                                           /        \\\n    38\t                                         yes         no\n    39\t                                          ↓           ↓\n    40\t                                  Artifact Generator   Re-plan /\n    41\t                                          ↓           Human Review\n    42\t                                      Artifact GER\n    43\t                                          ↓\n    44\t                                  Approved Artifact\n    45\t                                          ↓\n    46\t                                  Back to Decomposer\n    47\t```\n    48\t\n    49\t## Assignment 4 RAG Role\n    50\t\n    51\tRAG answers:\n    52\t\n    53\t> What does the GDD say?\n    54\t\n    55\tIt should not answer whether implementation exists.\n    56\t\n    57\tReuse/wrap the Assignment 4 retriever rather than duplicating it when practical.\n    58\t\n    59\tPossible interface:\n    60\t\n    61\t```text\n    62\tpython -m gddctl search \"mana regeneration\"\n    63\tpython -m gddctl requirement GDD-MANA-001\n    64\t```\n    65\t\n    66\t## Requirement Records\n    67\t\n    68\tWork items should eventually reference stable requirement IDs such as:\n    69\t\n    70\t```yaml\n    71\tsource_requirements:\n    72\t  - GDD-MANA-001\n    73\t  - GDD-SPELL-001\n    74\t```\n    75\t\n    76\tA requirement record should retain:\n    77\t\n    78\t- ID\n    79\t- source location/chunk\n    80\t- canonical description/text\n    81\t- optional tags\n    82\t- GDD version/hash\n    83\t\n    84\t## Approved Artifact Retrieval\n    85\t\n    86\tThe context builder must also be able to retrieve approved design artifacts that are dependencies or descendants of the current work.\n    87\t\n    88\tApproved artifacts are subordinate project design state.\n    89\t\n    90\tThey may add authorized detail but may not override contradictory GDD canon.\n    91\t\n    92\tUnapproved drafts must never be included as trusted context.\n    93\t\n    94\t## Local Unity/Code Scanner\n    95\t\n    96\tStart deterministic and simple:\n    97\t\n    98\t- filesystem enumeration\n    99\t- ripgrep\n   100\t- Unity YAML parsing\n   101\t- `.meta` GUID mapping\n   102\t- Roslyn later if useful\n   103\t\n   104\tQuestions it should eventually answer include:\n   105\t\n   106\t- whether a class exists\n   107\t- where a type is referenced\n   108\t- whether Grid/Tilemap/NavMesh components exist\n   109\t- whether a Camera is orthographic\n   110\t- which scene/prefab references a MonoBehaviour GUID\n   111\t- whether something is only builder capability versus serialized state\n   112\t- whether tests exist for a feature\n   113\t- whether a candidate \"complete\" task is actually integrated on `main`\n   114\t\n   115\t## Important State Distinction\n   116\t\n   117\tPreserve the Assignment 5 lesson:\n   118\t\n   119\t```text\n   120\tDEFINED\n   121\tCREATED_BY_BUILDER\n   122\tSERIALIZED_IN_SCENE\n   123\tATTACHED_TO_PREFAB\n   124\t```\n   125\t\n   126\tA builder method that can create something is not proof it currently exists.\n   127\t\n   128\t## Project State Cache\n   129\t\n   130\tStore output such as:\n   131\t\n   132\t```text\n   133\t.cache/project_state.json\n   134\t```\n   135\t\n   136\tIncremental scanning can come later.\n   137\t\n   138\t## Context Builder\n   139\t\n   140\tCreate something like:\n   141\t\n   142\t```text\n   143\tpython -m taskcontrol context NSC-014\n   144\t```\n   145\t\n   146\tOutput should contain only:\n   147\t\n   148\t- work contract\n   149\t- work kind\n   150\t- parent/feature context\n   151\t- acceptance criteria\n   152\t- out-of-scope boundaries\n   153\t- relevant GDD chunks\n   154\t- relevant approved artifact content\n   155\t- targeted current-project findings\n   156\t- relevant file paths\n   157\t\n   158\tDo not send the whole GDD/repo.\n   159\t\n   160\t## Progressive Decomposer\n   161\t\n   162\tClaude is used for semantic decomposition, not dependency bookkeeping.\n   163\t\n   164\tGiven one near-frontier feature/work item plus its relevant canon, approved artifacts, and current project evidence, the Decomposer first identifies **why** the item is not ready for a one-agent handoff.\n   165\t\n   166\tThere are two different modes:\n   167\t\n   168\t1. **Design decomposition** — `decomposition_state` is too coarse / missing approved design. If information is missing, identify the smallest missing design/content artifact needed to continue decomposition.\n   169\t2. **Execution decomposition** — design is already concrete, but `execution_scope: needs_execution_decomposition`. Split only the already-approved implementation responsibilities into smaller independently verifiable child work. Do not invent new mechanics/content.\n   170\t\n   171\tIf enough design exists and the work is already bounded, it can remain/transition to `execution_scope: single_agent`.\n   172\t\n   173\tIt must not generate that missing artifact during the same decision.\n   174\t\n   175\tDetecting missing design and creating missing design are separate actions.\n   176\t\n   177\t### Decomposer Output\n   178\t\n   179\tConceptually:\n   180\t\n   181\t```json\n   182\t{\n   183\t  \"work_id\": \"NSC-130\",\n   184\t  \"result\": \"needs_artifact\",\n   185\t  \"reason\": \"Room 3 cannot be implemented because encounter structure is undefined.\",\n   186\t  \"proposed_artifact\": {\n   187\t    \"title\": \"Room 3 Encounter Specification\",\n   188\t    \"purpose\": \"Define enough authorized encounter detail to create implementation tasks.\",\n   189\t    \"source_requirements\": [\n   190\t      \"GDD-WORLD-001\",\n   191\t      \"GDD-ENCOUNTER-001\"\n   192\t    ]\n   193\t  }\n   194\t}\n   195\t```\n   196\t\n   197\tor:\n   198\t\n   199\t```json\n   200\t{\n   201\t  \"work_id\": \"NSC-130\",\n   202\t  \"result\": \"decomposed\",\n   203\t  \"children\": [\n   204\t    {\n   205\t      \"kind\": \"implementation\",\n   206\t      \"title\": \"Build Room 3 Layout\"\n   207\t    },\n   208\t    {\n   209\t      \"kind\": \"implementation\",\n   210\t      \"title\": \"Configure Room 3 Enemies\"\n   211\t    }\n   212\t  ]\n   213\t}\n   214\t```\n   215\t\n   216\tThe final schema should be designed only when implementing this milestone.\n   217\t\n   218\t## Artifact Authority Gate\n   219\t\n   220\tBefore a proposed artifact may be generated, an evaluator determines whether creating that new information is justified.\n   221\t\n   222\tThe gate answers:\n   223\t\n   224\t1. Is this artifact actually necessary to progress the parent work?\n   225\t2. What existing GDD requirements or approved artifacts authorize its creation?\n   226\t3. What design decisions may the artifact make?\n   227\t4. What must it not invent, replace, or contradict?\n   228\t\n   229\tPossible outcomes:\n   230\t\n   231\t```text\n   232\tAUTHORIZED\n   233\tREJECTED\n   234\tNEEDS_HUMAN_REVIEW\n   235\t```\n   236\t\n   237\tA rejected artifact proposal returns to decomposition or human review.\n   238\t\n   239\tThe Authority Gate authorizes generation.\n   240\t\n   241\tIt does not judge the quality of the generated artifact.\n   242\t\n   243\t## Artifact Generation\n   244\t\n   245\tOnly authorized artifact proposals may be generated.\n   246\t\n   247\tGeneration should use:\n   248\t\n   249\t- relevant GDD canon from RAG\n   250\t- parent work context\n   251\t- relevant approved artifacts\n   252\t- explicit authority boundaries\n   253\t- explicit out-of-scope design areas\n   254\t\n   255\tArtifact generation should be bounded to the smallest missing design needed to continue decomposition.\n   256\t\n   257\tDo not create unnecessary lore, mechanics, factions, enemies, spells, or other design simply to make an artifact feel complete.\n   258\t\n   259\t## Artifact GER\n   260\t\n   261\tGenerated artifacts must pass appropriate post-generation evaluation before promotion to trusted project input.\n   262\t\n   263\tPossible evaluator profiles:\n   264\t\n   265\t```text\n   266\tCanon / Design Evaluator\n   267\tCompleteness Evaluator\n   268\tStyle Evaluator\n   269\tFormatting / Schema Evaluator\n   270\t```\n   271\t\n   272\tNot every artifact needs every evaluator.\n   273\t\n   274\t### Canon / Design Evaluator\n   275\t\n   276\tChecks whether the artifact:\n   277\t\n   278\t- stays within its granted authority\n   279\t- satisfies relevant GDD constraints\n   280\t- avoids unsupported design expansion\n   281\t- avoids contradiction with approved canon\n   282\t\n   283\t### Completeness Evaluator\n   284\t\n   285\tChecks whether the artifact contains enough concrete information for its intended downstream purpose.\n   286\t\n   287\tFor decomposition artifacts, the key question is:\n   288\t\n   289\t> Does this artifact now provide enough approved detail to produce bounded child work?\n   290\t\n   291\t### Style Evaluator — Assignment 7\n   292\t\n   293\tAssignment 7 supplies the scored style-checking specialization for style-sensitive generated content:\n   294\t\n   295\t```text\n   296\tGenerator\n   297\t  ↓\n   298\tStyle Evaluator\n   299\t  ↓\n   300\tSCORE + REASON\n   301\t  ↓\n   302\tRefiner\n   303\t  ↓\n   304\tStyle Evaluator\n   305\t```\n   306\t\n   307\tThis evaluator may check:\n   308\t\n   309\t- tone/vibe\n   310\t- No Safe Circle-specific vocabulary/canon\n   311\t- formatting conventions\n   312\t- length limits\n   313\t- other game-specific style constraints\n   314\t\n   315\tThe Style Evaluator does not decide whether the artifact was authorized to exist.\n   316\t\n   317\tAuthority comes first.\n   318\t\n   319\t## Artifact Promotion\n   320\t\n   321\tAfter required evaluators pass, the artifact becomes approved project input.\n   322\t\n   323\tConceptually:\n   324\t\n   325\t```text\n   326\tDesign/\n   327\t  Approved/\n   328\t    Encounters/\n   329\t      Room3.md\n   330\t```\n   331\t\n   332\tor another versioned project location.\n   333\t\n   334\tThe final directory/versioning strategy should be chosen during implementation.\n   335\t\n   336\tApproved artifact metadata should eventually retain:\n   337\t\n   338\t- artifact ID\n   339\t- parent work ID\n   340\t- source requirement IDs\n   341\t- authority decision\n   342\t- evaluator results\n   343\t- approval timestamp/version\n   344\t- file/hash\n   345\t\n   346\tDo not over-design this until the first artifact flow works.\n   347\t\n   348\t## Re-Decomposition\n   349\t\n   350\tAfter artifact approval:\n   351\t\n   352\t1. add/mark the artifact work as complete;\n   353\t2. rebuild the parent context pack;\n   354\t3. re-run the Progressive Decomposer;\n   355\t4. create concrete child work;\n   356\t5. validate the updated graph deterministically.\n   357\t\n   358\tThis is the core progressive-decomposition loop.\n   359\t\n   360\t## Token-Saving Goal\n   361\t\n   362\tReplace repeated full-project Claude crawls with:\n   363\t\n   364\t1. deterministic graph state;\n   365\t2. targeted RAG;\n   366\t3. targeted project scanning;\n   367\t4. one bounded design- or execution-decomposition call;\n   368\t5. artifact generation only when missing design actually blocks progress.\n   369\t\n   370\tThe system should not design Room 5 while still building Room 1 unless Room 5 design is truly needed by current work.\n   371\t\n   372\t## Completion Criteria\n   373\t\n   374\t1. Assignment 4 RAG can be queried locally.\n   375\t2. Project scanner produces deterministic state.\n   376\t3. Context builder combines work + canon + approved artifact + project evidence.\n   377\t4. Progressive Decomposer can distinguish design gaps (`needs_artifact`) from execution-size gaps (`needs_execution_decomposition`).\n   378\t5. Missing design produces an artifact proposal rather than silent invention.\n   379\t6. Artifact Authority Gate can authorize/reject/escalate proposals.\n   380\t7. Authorized artifacts can be generated and evaluated through GER.\n   381\t8. Approved artifacts can re-enter context as trusted design input.\n   382\t9. Re-running the Decomposer after artifact approval can produce concrete child work, and execution decomposition can split a concrete-but-oversized item without inventing design.\n   383\t10. Normal decomposition no longer requires a full GDD/repository crawl.\n   384\t\n   385\t## Next\n   386\t\n   387\tContinue with `03_SUPERVISOR_GIT_GITHUB_CONTEXT.md`.\n   388\t\n   389\tBy the end of this milestone, the persistent graph should be able to expand safely near the actionable frontier without allowing implementation agents to invent missing game design.\n   390\t\n   391\t## Reconciliation Boundary\n   392\t\n   393\tReconciliation snapshots remain immutable after creation.\n   394\t\n   395\tMilestone 2 context/decomposition may discover new work or new artifact\n   396\tdependencies, but those discoveries update/propose changes to the persistent\n   397\twork graph. They do not rewrite historical reconciliation snapshots.\n   398\t\n   399\tIf later repository/GDD drift requires a full reconciliation, that run creates\n   400\ta new snapshot and enters through the same deterministic graph-delta boundary.\n   401\t\n     1\t# Context 3 — Supervisor + Git Branches/Worktrees + GitHub Tickets/PRs\n     2\t\n     3\t## Goal\n     4\t\n     5\tCreate the first autonomous execution loop for **one ticket at a time**.\n     6\t\n     7\tPrerequisites: task graph, ready queue, and at least a minimal task context builder.\n     8\t\n     9\t## Critical Design Decision\n    10\t\n    11\tClaude does not own the autonomous loop. A local supervisor owns it and invokes Claude as a worker.\n    12\t\n    13\tThis allows project execution to resume after crashes, context loss, model session limits, and timeouts.\n    14\t\n    15\t## Git Strategy\n    16\t\n    17\tNever let autonomous workers modify `main` directly.\n    18\t\n    19\tFor a task such as NSC-014:\n    20\t\n    21\t```text\n    22\tbranch: claude/NSC-014-mana-resource\n    23\tworktree: .worktrees/NSC-014/\n    24\t```\n    25\t\n    26\tWorktrees isolate ticket work and prepare for future safe parallelism.\n    27\t\n    28\tDo **not** introduce parallel Claude workers yet.\n    29\t\n    30\t## GitHub Mapping\n    31\t\n    32\tEach local task should map to a GitHub Issue. GitHub is the human-facing dashboard.\n    33\t\n    34\tSuggested Project statuses:\n    35\t\n    36\t- Backlog\n    37\t- Ready\n    38\t- In Progress\n    39\t- Blocked\n    40\t- Validating\n    41\t- Needs Review\n    42\t- Done\n    43\t\n    44\tMirror task dependencies into GitHub when practical.\n    45\t\n    46\t## Source-of-Truth Split\n    47\t\n    48\tLocal task artifact owns durable definition: scope, dependencies, acceptance criteria, canon references.\n    49\t\n    50\tGitHub owns operational visibility: who/what is working, Draft PR, review state, blocked state, merge/close history.\n    51\t\n    52\tAvoid storing transient worker state in every Git branch.\n    53\t\n    54\t## Draft PR Early\n    55\t\n    56\tWhen a ticket is claimed:\n    57\t\n    58\t1. create branch/worktree\n    59\t2. mark GitHub Issue In Progress\n    60\t3. open Draft PR immediately\n    61\t4. launch Claude\n    62\t\n    63\tPR body should include ticket ID/title, scope, acceptance criteria, validation checklist, task-artifact reference, and a closing issue link/keyword when appropriate.\n    64\t\n    65\t## Commit Strategy\n    66\t\n    67\tClaude may make multiple working commits on its ticket branch, e.g.:\n    68\t\n    69\t```text\n    70\tNSC-014: Add PlayerMana component\n    71\tNSC-014: Add mana tests\n    72\tNSC-014: Fix regen delay behavior\n    73\t```\n    74\t\n    75\tEventually prefer squash merge into main so main contains one clean logical commit per completed ticket.\n    76\t\n    77\tA downstream dependency becomes satisfied only after the task is merged to main.\n    78\t\n    79\t## Supervisor First Version\n    80\t\n    81\t```text\n    82\t1. taskctl ready\n    83\t2. choose highest-ranked safe task\n    84\t3. claim it\n    85\t4. create branch/worktree\n    86\t5. open Draft PR\n    87\t6. build context\n    88\t7. run Claude\n    89\t8. collect result\n    90\t9. move to validation\n    91\t```\n    92\t\n    93\tDo not automate merge until validation is reliable.\n    94\t\n    95\t## Claims / Leases\n    96\t\n    97\tImplement a claim concept before parallelism:\n    98\t\n    99\t- task ID\n   100\t- worker ID\n   101\t- started time\n   102\t- expiration/heartbeat later\n   103\t\n   104\tTasks may eventually have conflict/resource claims, e.g. `System:PlayerResources` or `Scene:DoorPrototype`.\n   105\t\n   106\t## Failure Behavior\n   107\t\n   108\tIf Claude fails/times out:\n   109\t\n   110\t- preserve task, branch, PR, and logs\n   111\t- do not mark complete\n   112\t- retry only within configured limits\n   113\t- make supervisor restartable/idempotent\n   114\t\n   115\t## Completion Criteria\n   116\t\n   117\tOne real ticket can reliably go:\n   118\t\n   119\t```text\n   120\tReady → Claimed → Branch/Worktree → Draft PR → Claude implementation → commits visible → waiting for validation\n   121\t```\n   122\t\n   123\t## Next\n   124\t\n   125\tContinue with `04_EXECUTION_GER_VALIDATION_CONTEXT.md`.\n     1\t# Context 4 — Agent Crew + GER + Validation / Repair Loop\n     2\t\n     3\t## Goal\n     4\t\n     5\tTurn ticket implementation into a reliable bounded execution pipeline.\n     6\t\n     7\tPrerequisites: supervisor, worktree/branch/PR, and task context.\n     8\t\n     9\t## Assignment 3 Crew Placement\n    10\t\n    11\tThe crew belongs inside ticket execution:\n    12\t\n    13\t```text\n    14\tPersistent Task Graph chooses WHAT\n    15\t       ↓\n    16\tTicket Context\n    17\t       ↓\n    18\tPlanner\n    19\t       ↓\n    20\tImplementer\n    21\t       ↓\n    22\tValidator\n    23\t```\n    24\t\n    25\tDo not let the crew rebuild the global roadmap.\n    26\t\n    27\t## Planner\n    28\t\n    29\tInput: task artifact, acceptance criteria, relevant GDD chunks, relevant project state/files.\n    30\t\n    31\tOutput: small implementation plan, expected files, validation plan.\n    32\t\n    33\tNo scope broadening.\n    34\t\n    35\t## Implementer\n    36\t\n    37\tImplement only the selected ticket. If a new substantial prerequisite is discovered, stop and report a blocker instead of silently absorbing another ticket.\n    38\t\n    39\t## Deterministic Validation First\n    40\t\n    41\tRun local checks before asking another model:\n    42\t\n    43\t- `git diff --check`\n    44\t- prohibited-file/path checks\n    45\t- scope checks\n    46\t- static checks\n    47\t- task graph checks\n    48\t- unit tests\n    49\t- Unity compile\n    50\t- Unity EditMode tests\n    51\t- Unity PlayMode tests\n    52\t\n    53\t## Unity Validation\n    54\t\n    55\tEventually run Unity command-line/batch validation on the real development machine. Do not treat a Docker environment without Unity as final runtime truth.\n    56\t\n    57\tProduce machine-readable test results.\n    58\t\n    59\t## Fresh AI Diff Review\n    60\t\n    61\tAfter deterministic validation, give a fresh reviewer only:\n    62\t\n    63\t- task contract\n    64\t- acceptance criteria\n    65\t- relevant GDD evidence\n    66\t- git diff\n    67\t- deterministic test results\n    68\t\n    69\tAsk whether the diff satisfies the ticket without exceeding scope.\n    70\t\n    71\t## Repair Loop\n    72\t\n    73\t```text\n    74\tfailure evidence → Implementer repair → deterministic tests → AI diff review\n    75\t```\n    76\t\n    77\tLimit retries.\n    78\t\n    79\t## Assignment 6 — GER\n    80\t\n    81\tUse GER for generated game content/artifacts:\n    82\t\n    83\t```text\n    84\tGenerator\n    85\t ↓\n    86\tEvaluator\n    87\t ↓ PASS → approved artifact\n    88\t\n    89\tFAIL\n    90\t ↓\n    91\tRefiner\n    92\t ↓\n    93\tEvaluator\n    94\t ↓\n    95\t(retry limit)\n    96\t ↓\n    97\tCircuit Breaker\n    98\t ↓\n    99\tNeeds Human Review\n   100\t```\n   101\t\n   102\tRAG feeds the evaluator with exact GDD rules.\n   103\t\n   104\tPossible generated artifact directories:\n   105\t\n   106\t```text\n   107\tArtifacts/\n   108\t  Encounters/\n   109\t  ArtSpecs/\n   110\t  Tutorials/\n   111\t  Balance/\n   112\t  Dialogue/\n   113\t```\n   114\t\n   115\tA generated artifact can be a dependency of a later code/content task.\n   116\t\n   117\tExample:\n   118\t\n   119\t```text\n   120\tNSC-035 Generate Bone Archive Encounter\n   121\t        ↓\n   122\tapproved artifact\n   123\t        ↓\n   124\tNSC-040 Implement Bone Archive Encounter\n   125\t```\n   126\t\n   127\t## Cost / Circuit Breakers\n   128\t\n   129\tPer ticket define limits such as max agent runs, max repair attempts, runtime, and budget. Escalate on GDD ambiguity, repeated test/GER failure, architecture-changing dependencies, scope expansion, Unity conflicts, or budget exhaustion.\n   130\t\n   131\t## Completion Definition\n   132\t\n   133\t```text\n   134\timplementation → deterministic tests → Unity tests → fresh AI review → PR ready → merge → task complete\n   135\t```\n   136\t\n   137\tA worker saying “implemented” is not completion.\n   138\t\n   139\t## Completion Criteria\n   140\t\n   141\tA ticket reliably reaches either `Merged / Done` or `Needs Human Review`, with bounded spending and clear evidence.\n   142\t\n   143\t## Next\n   144\t\n   145\tContinue with `05_CONTINUOUS_AUTONOMY_CONTEXT.md`.\n     1\t# Context 5 — Continuous Autonomous Development\n     2\t\n     3\t## Goal\n     4\t\n     5\tOnce one-ticket execution is reliable, let the system continue through the safe ready backlog with minimal supervision.\n     6\t\n     7\t## Main Loop\n     8\t\n     9\t```python\n    10\twhile True:\n    11\t    ready = taskctl.ready_ranked()\n    12\t\n    13\t    if not ready:\n    14\t        run_planning_reconciliation_or_pause()\n    15\t        continue\n    16\t\n    17\t    task = select_first_safe_task(ready)\n    18\t    result = execute_ticket(task)\n    19\t\n    20\t    if result == \"merged\":\n    21\t        taskctl.recalculate()\n    22\t        continue\n    23\t\n    24\t    if result == \"blocked\":\n    25\t        record_blocker()\n    26\t        continue_with_another_ready_task()\n    27\t\n    28\t    if result == \"needs_human\":\n    29\t        surface_to_user()\n    30\t        continue_with_other_safe_work_if_allowed()\n    31\t```\n    32\t\n    33\t## Principle\n    34\t\n    35\tThe supervisor is autonomous. Claude is a bounded/replaceable worker per ticket.\n    36\t\n    37\t## Newly Discovered Blockers\n    38\t\n    39\tIf Fireball discovers a substantial missing Projectile Collision prerequisite:\n    40\t\n    41\t1. report blocker\n    42\t2. propose/create a new task\n    43\t3. validate/add dependency\n    44\t4. mark Fireball blocked\n    45\t5. preserve its branch/PR if useful\n    46\t6. scheduler chooses another safe ready task\n    47\t\n    48\tDo not silently implement both goals.\n    49\t\n    50\t## Planning / Backlog Replenishment\n    51\t\n    52\tWhen the ready queue is empty or backlog coverage is low:\n    53\t\n    54\t```text\n    55\tGDD RAG + current project scanner + existing tasks\n    56\t ↓\n    57\trequirements reconciliation\n    58\t ↓\n    59\tproposed task artifacts/dependencies\n    60\t ↓\n    61\tgraph validation\n    62\t ↓\n    63\tplanning PR\n    64\t```\n    65\t\n    66\tInitially require human approval for roadmap/task-graph changes.\n    67\t\n    68\t## GDD Change Detection\n    69\t\n    70\tTrack a GDD hash/version. On material changes, invalidate relevant caches and reconcile affected requirements/tasks rather than blindly rebuilding everything.\n    71\t\n    72\t## GitHub Dashboard\n    73\t\n    74\tThe user should be able to see Backlog, Ready, In Progress, Blocked, Validating, Needs Review, and Done, with an Issue/PR/dependency/validation trail for active work.\n    75\t\n    76\t## Merge Safety\n    77\t\n    78\tBefore final validation, update the ticket branch against current main and rerun validation. Consider a merge queue only once multiple workers exist.\n    79\t\n    80\t## Parallelism\n    81\t\n    82\tOnly after single-worker reliability. Scheduler must reject overlapping conflict/resource claims. Be conservative with Unity scenes, prefabs, ProjectSettings, and shared builder scripts.\n    83\t\n    84\t## Observability\n    85\t\n    86\tPersist run records outside model context:\n    87\t\n    88\t```text\n    89\tRuns/\n    90\t  NSC-014/\n    91\t    <run-id>/\n    92\t```\n    93\t\n    94\tRecord task ID, worker/model, timestamps, context hash, token/cost usage if available, commits, tests, retries, errors, and disposition.\n    95\t\n    96\t## Budget Governance\n    97\t\n    98\tAdd project-level limits as well as ticket limits, e.g. daily budget, max concurrent workers, and pause thresholds for accumulated human-review items.\n    99\t\n   100\t## Human Intervention Queue\n   101\t\n   102\tQuestions should be small and concrete. Once the human makes an architecture/design decision, persist it so agents do not ask the same question repeatedly.\n   103\t\n   104\t## Long-Term Desired Experience\n   105\t\n   106\tThe user should be able to say:\n   107\t\n   108\t> Work through No Safe Circle's safe ready backlog until you need me.\n   109\t\n   110\tThen monitor GitHub to see what Claude is doing, what finished, what is blocked, what needs review, and what became ready next.\n   111\t\n   112\t## Definition of Success\n   113\t\n   114\t1. Project state survives chat/model restarts.\n   115\t2. Claude receives small bounded contexts.\n   116\t3. Dependencies are deterministic.\n   117\t4. Code is isolated by branch/worktree.\n   118\t5. Tests gate completion.\n   119\t6. Merge defines Done.\n   120\t7. Failures are bounded and observable.\n   121\t8. System moves to another ready task without human prompting.\n   122\t9. Roadmap changes are deliberate, not LLM drift.\n   123\t10. Token/cost use is much lower than repeated full-repository reasoning.\n     1\t# Architecture Decisions — No Safe Circle AI Pipeline\r\n     2\t\r\n     3\tThis is a lightweight decision log. Add new entries when a decision materially changes how the pipeline operates.\r\n     4\t\r\n     5\t---\r\n     6\t\r\n     7\t## ADR-001 — Repository state beats conversation memory\r\n     8\t\r\n     9\t**Decision:** Persistent pipeline state, work definitions, dependencies, and architectural documentation live in the repository.\r\n    10\t\r\n    11\t**Reason:** AI chat contexts are temporary and may not contain all prior detail.\r\n    12\t\r\n    13\t---\r\n    14\t\r\n    15\t## ADR-002 — Local supervisor owns autonomy\r\n    16\t\r\n    17\t**Decision:** Claude does not own the infinite/continuous loop. A local deterministic supervisor chooses and launches bounded worker executions.\r\n    18\t\r\n    19\t**Reason:** This allows recovery from session limits, failures, restarts, and cost limits.\r\n    20\t\r\n    21\t---\r\n    22\t\r\n    23\t## ADR-003 — Claude receives one bounded ticket\r\n    24\t\r\n    25\t**Decision:** Normal coding workers receive one work contract rather than the whole project roadmap.\r\n    26\t\r\n    27\t**Reason:** Reduces token use, scope drift, and repeated rediscovery.\r\n    28\t\r\n    29\t---\r\n    30\t\r\n    31\t## ADR-004 — Task dependency graph is persistent and local\r\n    32\t\r\n    33\t**Decision:** Work items are stored as local artifacts with explicit dependencies. Local code calculates ready/actionable work.\r\n    34\t\r\n    35\t**Reason:** Dependency readiness is computation, not something an LLM should reconstruct on every run.\r\n    36\t\r\n    37\t---\r\n    38\t\r\n    39\t## ADR-005 — GitHub is the dashboard, not the project brain\r\n    40\t\r\n    41\t**Decision:** Local/versioned work artifacts define durable work. GitHub Issues/Projects/PRs mirror operational state and provide human visibility.\r\n    42\t\r\n    43\t**Reason:** Workers need fast deterministic local access, while the user needs a convenient remote dashboard.\r\n    44\t\r\n    45\t---\r\n    46\t\r\n    47\t## ADR-006 — `main` is protected from autonomous direct development\r\n    48\t\r\n    49\t**Decision:** Each coding ticket gets its own branch and preferably its own Git worktree.\r\n    50\t\r\n    51\t**Reason:** Provides isolation, reviewability, recovery, and a path to future parallelism.\r\n    52\t\r\n    53\t---\r\n    54\t\r\n    55\t## ADR-007 — Done means merged\r\n    56\t\r\n    57\t**Decision:** An implementation task satisfies downstream dependencies only after its implementation is merged into `main`.\r\n    58\t\r\n    59\t**Reason:** Code existing on an unmerged worker branch is not project state.\r\n    60\t\r\n    61\t---\r\n    62\t\r\n    63\t## ADR-008 — RAG is the canon retrieval layer\r\n    64\t\r\n    65\t**Decision:** Assignment 4 RAG retrieves relevant GDD evidence for work instead of repeatedly sending the entire GDD to an LLM.\r\n    66\t\r\n    67\t**Reason:** Reduces context size and keeps generated work grounded in canonical design information.\r\n    68\t\r\n    69\t---\r\n    70\t\r\n    71\t## ADR-009 — Assignment 3 crew executes tickets\r\n    72\t\r\n    73\t**Decision:** Planner → Implementer → Validator operates inside one selected bounded implementation task.\r\n    74\t\r\n    75\t**Reason:** The persistent graph decides WHAT to build; the crew decides HOW to build that bounded work.\r\n    76\t\r\n    77\t---\r\n    78\t\r\n    79\t## ADR-010 — GER is the bounded self-correction loop for project work\r\n    80\t\r\n    81\t**Decision:** Assignment 6 GER (Generator/Implementer → Evaluator → Refiner → Circuit Breaker) is used as the general bounded repair pattern for work that can be evaluated and refined, including Unity implementation code and generated game/content artifacts.\r\n    82\t\r\n    83\t**Reason:** Assignment 6 successfully used GER to implement and repair the isometric camera. The architecture therefore should not restrict GER to generated content. The useful abstraction is a bounded work product with explicit acceptance evidence, structured failure feedback, refinement, and escalation.\r\n    84\t\r\n    85\t**Supersedes:** The earlier wording \"GER gates generated artifacts.\"\r\n    86\t\r\n    87\t---\r\n    88\t\r\n    89\t## ADR-011 — Cheap deterministic validation should run before expensive semantic review when practical\r\n    90\t\r\n    91\t**Decision:** Git checks, scope checks, static checks, Unity compilation/tests, and other deterministic validation should detect cheap/objective failures before spending model tokens on fresh semantic review whenever the work allows it.\r\n    92\t\r\n    93\t**Reason:** Do not spend model tokens on failures a local tool can detect.\r\n    94\t\r\n    95\t**Clarification:** This does not mean deterministic checks alone can approve interactive Unity behavior.\r\n    96\t\r\n    97\t---\r\n    98\t\r\n    99\t## ADR-012 — Newly discovered substantial work becomes a task\r\n   100\t\r\n   101\t**Decision:** A worker that discovers a substantial independently-testable prerequisite must stop/block rather than silently absorb it.\r\n   102\t\r\n   103\t**Reason:** Preserves work scope and keeps the dependency graph truthful.\r\n   104\t\r\n   105\t---\r\n   106\t\r\n   107\t## ADR-013 — Start with one worker\r\n   108\t\r\n   109\t**Decision:** Continuous parallel Claude workers are deferred until one-ticket execution is reliable.\r\n   110\t\r\n   111\t**Reason:** Unity scenes, prefabs, `.meta` files, and shared systems can create expensive merge conflicts.\r\n   112\t\r\n   113\t---\r\n   114\t\r\n   115\t## ADR-014 — Every autonomous loop has budgets/circuit breakers\r\n   116\t\r\n   117\t**Decision:** Ticket execution, repair, artifact generation, and GER loops have runtime/retry/cost limits and escalate when exceeded.\r\n   118\t\r\n   119\t**Reason:** Autonomous must mean bounded safe progress, not unlimited token spending.\r\n   120\t\r\n   121\t---\r\n   122\t\r\n   123\t## ADR-015 — Runtime evidence is first-class validation input\r\n   124\t\r\n   125\t**Decision:** For visual, interactive, timing-sensitive, or scene-dependent Unity behavior, runtime evidence must be able to fail a task even when static/GDD evaluation passes. Runtime failures are converted into structured feedback and re-enter the Refiner loop.\r\n   126\t\r\n   127\t**Reason:** Assignment 6's camera passed static evaluation while facing the wrong direction, lacking usable following behavior, and later framing the game incorrectly. Unity runtime testing found defects the source-level evaluator did not.\r\n   128\t\r\n   129\t---\r\n   130\t\r\n   131\t## ADR-016 — A no-op refinement is failed progress\r\n   132\t\r\n   133\t**Decision:** If unresolved failures remain and a Refiner produces no relevant project change, that attempt is counted as a failed repair and consumes repair budget.\r\n   134\t\r\n   135\t**Reason:** Assignment 6 observed a Refiner reporting `implemented` while making no changes under `Assets/`. Accepting that result would allow false success or an infinite repair loop.\r\n   136\t\r\n   137\t---\r\n   138\t\r\n   139\t## ADR-017 — Validation should produce an evidence/feedback bundle\r\n   140\t\r\n   141\t**Decision:** Work validation should aggregate required deterministic, Unity/runtime, and semantic evidence into a form that can either approve work or produce structured Refiner feedback.\r\n   142\t\r\n   143\t**Reason:** Assignment 6 succeeded once Unity observations could be fed back into the same repair loop. A single evaluator result is too narrow for many game-development tasks.\r\n   144\t\r\n   145\t---\r\n   146\t\r\n   147\t## ADR-018 — Old goal-selection artifacts are not current codebase truth\r\n   148\t\r\n   149\t**Decision:** Saved Assignment 5 planning/goal-selection output may be reused as historical evidence, but completion and readiness must be reconciled against the current `main` branch before seeding the durable dependency graph.\r\n   150\t\r\n   151\t**Reason:** Assignment outputs can become stale as features are merged or changed. Persistent project state must reflect the repository that actually ships.\r\n   152\t\r\n   153\t---\r\n   154\t\r\n   155\t## ADR-019 — The pipeline must advance the game while it is being built\r\n   156\t\r\n   157\t**Decision:** After the minimum deterministic work graph exists, pipeline work should be exercised on real ready No Safe Circle gameplay work rather than waiting for the entire autonomous platform to be finished.\r\n   158\t\r\n   159\t**Reason:** The pipeline is infrastructure for developing No Safe Circle, not a separate capstone that should indefinitely block game progress. Real work will expose the next infrastructure requirements more accurately than speculative over-building.\r\n   160\t\r\n   161\t---\r\n   162\t\r\n   163\t## ADR-020 — Style evaluation is an evaluator specialization, not a separate architecture\r\n   164\t\r\n   165\t**Decision:** When Assignment 7/player-facing content is ready, style enforcement should plug into GER as a specialized scored evaluator that returns `SCORE + REASON` and feeds the Refiner.\r\n   166\t\r\n   167\t**Reason:** Assignment 7 uses the same Generator → Evaluator → Refiner structure already proven by Assignment 6. Reusing the execution architecture avoids parallel pipelines and allows style checks to remain tied to actual No Safe Circle content.\r\n   168\t\r\n   169\t**Clarification:** Style evaluation does not authorize creation of new canon or design content. Artifact creation must already have passed the Artifact Authority Gate before the Style Evaluator is used.\r\n   170\t\r\n   171\t---\r\n   172\t\r\n   173\t## ADR-021 — Task decomposition is progressive and just-in-time\r\n   174\t\r\n   175\t**Decision:** The pipeline does not attempt to fully decompose the entire capstone into low-level implementation tickets in advance. High-level work is expanded as it approaches the actionable frontier.\r\n   176\t\r\n   177\t**Reason:** Distant work often lacks design information, and premature decomposition either wastes LLM reasoning or causes invented requirements.\r\n   178\t\r\n   179\t---\r\n   180\t\r\n   181\t## ADR-022 — Missing design creates an artifact proposal, not silent invention\r\n   182\t\r\n   183\t**Decision:** When work cannot be concretely decomposed because required design/content is missing, the Decomposer proposes a new artifact dependency instead of inventing the missing information during implementation.\r\n   184\t\r\n   185\t**Reason:** Implementation agents must not silently become game designers or turn hallucinated decisions into project requirements.\r\n   186\t\r\n   187\t---\r\n   188\t\r\n   189\t## ADR-023 — Generated design requires authority before generation\r\n   190\t\r\n   191\t**Decision:** A proposed design/content artifact must pass an authority check before generation. The check identifies why the artifact is needed, which existing requirements permit expansion, and which areas are outside its authority.\r\n   192\t\r\n   193\t**Reason:** Evaluating whether generated content is good is different from deciding whether the AI was allowed to invent that content in the first place.\r\n   194\t\r\n   195\t---\r\n   196\t\r\n   197\t## ADR-024 — Approved artifacts may become subordinate project design state\r\n   198\t\r\n   199\t**Decision:** After passing authority and required post-generation evaluators, an approved artifact may become trusted downstream design input subordinate to the GDD.\r\n   200\t\r\n   201\t**Reason:** Progressive decomposition needs a durable way to carry forward authorized design decisions without forcing later agents to regenerate or reinterpret them.\r\n   202\t\r\n   203\t**Constraint:** Approved artifacts may add detail where canon permits expansion but may not contradict or silently replace GDD canon.\r\n   204\t\r\n   205\t---\r\n   206\t\r\n   207\t## ADR-025 — Reconciliation results are immutable point-in-time snapshots\r\n   208\t\r\n   209\t**Decision:** Every full Reconciliation Agent run creates a new versioned snapshot. A later run never overwrites an earlier reconciliation snapshot, and later gameplay progress never edits old reconciliation results.\r\n   210\t\r\n   211\t**Reason:** Reconciliation answers what the GDD and integrated repository looked like at one moment. Keeping that evidence immutable preserves an audit trail and prevents historical observations from being confused with the living operational work state.\r\n   212\t\r\n   213\t**Implementation note:** `Pipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/LATEST.json` may be overwritten as a convenience pointer, but the snapshot directory it references is append-only.\r\n   214\t\r\n   215\t---\r\n   216\t\r\n   217\t## ADR-026 — Reconciliation proposes graph deltas but never directly mutates the persistent graph\r\n   218\t\r\n   219\t**Decision:** Reconciliation output is compared against `Tasks/*.yaml` through a deterministic diff/review/apply boundary. The Reconciliation Agent may propose new, changed, conflicting, or stale work records, but it does not rewrite the persistent graph itself.\r\n   220\t\r\n   221\t**Reason:** A new LLM interpretation must not trigger uncontrolled cascading edits across the project's operational state. Deterministic graph logic should own dependency satisfaction, readiness propagation, status derivation, and application of approved reconciliation changes.\r\n   222\t\r\n   223\t**Bootstrap clarification:** Before `Tasks/*.yaml` exists, reconciliation emits proposed seed records. Human approval plus the deterministic Work Graph Seeder turns those records into the initial graph.\r\n   224\t\r\n   225\t\r\n   226\t\r\n   227\t---\r\n   228\t\r\n   229\t## ADR-027 — Reconciliation bootstrap requires independent multi-model verification\r\n   230\t\r\n   231\t**Decision:** A reconciliation snapshot is not eligible for bootstrap seeding merely because the Generator and deterministic schema/graph validators succeeded. Before initial graph seeding, the candidate must be independently audited for GDD coverage, dependency/decomposition structure, repository evidence, and execution-scope suitability. At least two coverage audits use different requested Claude models when the configured model pool permits it.\r\n   232\t\r\n   233\t**Reason:** Deterministic validators catch structural defects but cannot prove semantic completeness. A single model can correctly notice a requirement yet still bury it inside the wrong work item, omit a reusable required capability, or accept its own evidence framing. Independent role-specialized audits reduce correlated semantic failure.\r\n   234\t\r\n   235\t**Finding policy:** Verifier findings are unioned, not majority-voted. One credible blocker/error must be resolved or explicitly escalated for human review.\r\n   236\t\r\n   237\t**Refinement policy:** Material findings may produce a bounded refined candidate, but the original reconciliation snapshot remains immutable. The refined candidate is independently re-verified before human approval.\r\n   238\t\r\n   239\t**Model policy:** Requested model assignments and the random assignment seed are saved with each verification run. Model diversity is an error-discovery technique, not a replacement for deterministic checks or the human approval gate.\r\n   240\t\r\n   241\t\r\n   242\t## ADR-029 — Design decomposition and execution scope are separate axes\r\n   243\t\r\n   244\t**Decision:** Every durable work item distinguishes whether the approved design is sufficiently concrete (`decomposition_state`) from whether the implementation work is a safe bounded one-agent handoff (`execution_scope`).\r\n   245\t\r\n   246\t**Why:** A requirement can be fully designed while still bundling multiple systems, files, integrations, or independently verifiable outcomes. Treating `concrete` as automatically executable would let `taskcontrol ready` hand an oversized task to one agent.\r\n   247\t\r\n   248\t**Consequence:** `taskcontrol ready` eventually returns only open artifact/implementation work with complete dependencies and `execution_scope: single_agent`. `needs_execution_decomposition`, `human_integration_required`, and `unknown` remain non-autonomous states. The Progressive Decomposer may later split concrete implementation responsibilities without inventing new design.\r\n   249\t\r\n   250\t## ADR-030 — Current output view is mutable; run history stays nested and immutable\r\n   251\t\r\n   252\t**Decision:** `Pipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/current/` is the human-facing convenience view. Reconciliation history remains under `outputs/runs/<run-id>/`, and new verification history lives under `outputs/runs/<run-id>/verifications/<verification-id>/`.\r\n   253\t\r\n   254\t**Why:** Separate top-level reconciliation and verification trees made it difficult to know which files were the current answer and which verification belonged to which source snapshot.\r\n   255\t\r\n   256\t**Consequence:** `current/` may be overwritten whenever the latest candidate changes. It is never historical truth. Existing legacy `outputs/verifications/` directories can be moved once with the deterministic layout-migration utility.\r\n   257\t\r\n   258\t---\r\n   259\t\r\n   260\t## ADR-035 — Provider-adapter enforcement\r\n   261\t\r\n   262\t**Accepted:** 2026-08-23. **Amended:** 2026-08-23 after provider-transport evaluation.\r\n   263\t\r\n   264\t**Decision:** Accept ADR-035's fail-closed initial provider boundaries while keeping `AgentRequest` schema 1.0 and its required positive integer `turn_limit`. Unsupported capabilities and budgets are rejected unless ADR-035 explicitly defines the provider-specific enforcement translation. Initial Codex support is limited to empty capabilities and empty `context_paths` in a new empty temporary workspace. Initial repository writing and approved command execution remain unsupported.\r\n   265\t\r\n   266\t**Turn-budget policy:** Claude maps `turn_limit=N` to native `--max-turns N` plus the independent external `timeout_seconds` ceiling. Codex has no observed native turn-limit flag, so it temporarily maps each requested turn-budget unit to 30 wall-clock seconds and uses `min(timeout_seconds, turn_limit * 30)` as its hard external deadline. These mappings bound execution and are not cross-provider-equivalent units of model work.\r\n   267\t\r\n   268\t**Output and error policy:** `provider.log` is exact stdout, successful stderr is empty, malformed provider output raises provider-neutral `ProviderOutputInvalid` normalized to `schema_error`, and `ProviderOutputInvalid` is now the only remaining AgentRuntime prerequisite before live adapter implementation. A non-null `token_limit` remains unsupported and fails closed. There is no automatic fallback.\r\n   269\t\r\n   270\t**Authority:** Deterministic Git, Unity, TaskGraph, evidence, readiness, dispatch, and human authority are unchanged.\r\n   271\t\r\n   272\t---\r\n   273\t\r\n   274\t## ADR-036 — Own the narrow Claude Code and OpenAI/Codex transport adapters\n   275\t\r\n   276\t**Decision:** Do not adopt `agent-mux` or `agent-shell` as a production transport dependency for Stage 4. Implement the two narrow provider adapters directly under `Pipeline/AgentRuntime/providers/` while reusing general implementation ideas that do not weaken project policy.\r\n   277\t\r\n   278\t**Reason:** A bounded adopt-versus-build spike found that neither library matches the already-approved fail-closed contract closely enough to remove meaningful implementation work. The tested `agent-mux` source commit required a Linux build patch and its built-in Claude/Codex argument contracts differ from the project's required structured-output and isolation flags. `agent-shell` contains useful Python process/streaming patterns but intentionally warns and continues for some unsupported tool restrictions rather than failing closed.\r\n   279\t\r\n   280\t**Allowed reuse:** General patterns such as process-group cleanup, concurrent stdout/stderr draining, external timeout supervision, provider-event parsing, and model/usage extraction may be adapted after review.\r\n   281\t\r\n   282\t**Not allowed:** A third-party transport may not become an authority layer, silently weaken capabilities, change the selected model, broaden filesystem/tool access, or substitute its own completion claims for deterministic Git/Unity/TaskGraph evidence.\r\n   283\t\r\n   284\t**Consequence:** The project owns CLI compatibility and parsing for two provider adapters, but avoids a larger dependency or fork whose policy surface would still require custom hardening.\n   285\t\n   286\t---\n   287\t\n   288\t## ADR-039 — Separate generic invocation from NSC task execution\n   289\t\n   290\t**Accepted:** 2026-08-23.\n   291\t\n   292\t**Decision:** `AgentRuntime` owns one generic bounded provider invocation through `AgentInvocationRequest`; it does not know NSC task IDs, task-contract identity, or `Tasks/*.yaml`. `Pipeline/TaskExecution` owns `TaskExecutionRequest` and delegates the exact contained invocation downward while retaining a separate immutable task audit artifact.\n   293\t\n   294\t**Reason:** Generic workflows such as ArchitectureReview and Reconciliation must be able to use AgentRuntime honestly, while ExecutionCrew task work must retain NSC task semantics and audit identity without contaminating the provider-neutral layer.\n   295\t\n   296\t---\n   297\t\n   298\t## ADR-040 — OpenAI/Codex AgentRuntime and ArchitectureReview migration\n   299\t\n   300\t**Accepted:** 2026-08-23.\n   301\t\n   302\t**Decision:** Implement OpenAI/Codex behind generic AgentRuntime and migrate the active OpenAI ArchitectureReview path through `AgentInvocationRequest -> AgentRunner -> OpenAICodexProvider`. Repository read/search relies on the explicit externally read-only `codex-review` Docker profile. TaskExecution, repository writes, approved command execution, and provider fallback are not involved.\n   303\t\n   304\t**Reason:** A real existing non-task consumer proves the corrected provider-neutral invocation boundary without inventing task identity or prematurely building ExecutionCrew.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
