{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba ProjectSettings/EditorBuildSettings.asset | sed -n '1,100p'; nl -ba Assets/NoSafeCircle/DoorPrototype/Editor/DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder.cs | sed -n '1,110p'; nl -ba Docs/AI-Pipeline/CURRENT_STATE.md | sed -n '1,110p'; nl -ba Docs/AI-Pipeline/CURRENT_STATE.md | sed -n '575,735p'; nl -ba Docs/AI-Pipeline/START_HERE.md | sed -n '1,70p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t%YAML 1.1\n     2\t%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:\n     3\t--- !u!1045 &1\n     4\tEditorBuildSettings:\n     5\t  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0\n     6\t  serializedVersion: 2\n     7\t  m_Scenes: []\n     8\t  m_configObjects:\n     9\t    com.unity.input.settings.actions: {fileID: -944628639613478452, guid: 289c1b55c9541489481df5cc06664110, type: 3}\n    10\t  m_UseUCBPForAssetBundles: 0\n     1\tusing System.IO;\r\n     2\tusing UnityEditor;\r\n     3\tusing UnityEditor.Events;\r\n     4\tusing UnityEditor.SceneManagement;\r\n     5\tusing UnityEngine;\r\n     6\tusing UnityEngine.EventSystems;\r\n     7\tusing UnityEngine.InputSystem.UI;\r\n     8\tusing UnityEngine.SceneManagement;\r\n     9\tusing UnityEngine.UI;\r\n    10\tusing Object = UnityEngine.Object;\r\n    11\t\r\n    12\tnamespace NoSafeCircle.DoorPrototype.Editor\r\n    13\t{\r\n    14\t    public static class DoorPrototypeSceneBuilder\r\n    15\t    {\r\n    16\t        private const string SceneFolder = \"Assets/Scenes\";\r\n    17\t        private const string ScenePath = SceneFolder + \"/DoorPrototype.unity\";\r\n    18\t\r\n    19\t        // Classic 2:1 dimetric isometric camera angle (rotate -45 degrees around Y to face\r\n    20\t        // a corner, then tilt 30 degrees down) matching Diablo 1 / Ultima Online-style\r\n    21\t        // fixed isometric presentation.\r\n    22\t        private static readonly Vector3 IsometricCameraEulerAngles = new Vector3(30f, -45f, 0f);\r\n    23\t\r\n    24\t        // Fixed, hand-picked world-space offset from the follow target to the camera. This is\r\n    25\t        // a plain constant - NOT derived by rotating a local vector through the camera's own\r\n    26\t        // rotation - so the camera's framing is decoupled from its orientation. Paired with\r\n    27\t        // IsometricCameraEulerAngles above, it keeps the player and the starting door in view\r\n    28\t        // from the intended side rather than merely pointing camera.forward at the player.\r\n    29\t        private static readonly Vector3 IsometricCameraOffset = new Vector3(10f, 10f, -10f);\r\n    30\t\r\n    31\t        private const float IsometricOrthographicSize = 8f;\r\n    32\t\r\n    33\t        private static readonly string[] KnownRootNames =\r\n    34\t        {\r\n    35\t            \"Directional Light\",\r\n    36\t            \"Main Camera\",\r\n    37\t            \"Floor\",\r\n    38\t            \"Walls\",\r\n    39\t            \"DoorRoot\",\r\n    40\t            \"Player\",\r\n    41\t            \"Canvas\",\r\n    42\t            \"EventSystem\"\r\n    43\t        };\r\n    44\t\r\n    45\t        [MenuItem(\"No Safe Circle/Build Door Prototype Scene\")]\r\n    46\t        public static void Build()\r\n    47\t        {\r\n    48\t            EnsureFolder(SceneFolder);\r\n    49\t\r\n    50\t            var scene = File.Exists(ScenePath)\r\n    51\t                ? EditorSceneManager.OpenScene(ScenePath, OpenSceneMode.Single)\r\n    52\t                : EditorSceneManager.NewScene(NewSceneSetup.EmptyScene, NewSceneMode.Single);\r\n    53\t\r\n    54\t            RebuildSceneContents(scene);\r\n    55\t\r\n    56\t            EditorSceneManager.MarkSceneDirty(scene);\r\n    57\t            EditorSceneManager.SaveScene(scene, ScenePath);\r\n    58\t            AssetDatabase.Refresh();\r\n    59\t\r\n    60\t            Debug.Log($\"Door Prototype scene built at {ScenePath}\");\r\n    61\t        }\r\n    62\t\r\n    63\t        // Test-only seam: rebuilds the current in-memory Editor scene without opening,\r\n    64\t        // saving, or refreshing any asset. Production Build() delegates to the same\r\n    65\t        // construction path before performing its normal canonical-scene save.\r\n    66\t        public static void BuildInMemoryForTests()\r\n    67\t        {\r\n    68\t            RebuildSceneContents(SceneManager.GetActiveScene());\r\n    69\t        }\r\n    70\t\r\n    71\t        private static void RebuildSceneContents(Scene scene)\r\n    72\t        {\r\n    73\t            ClearExistingObjects(scene);\r\n    74\t\r\n    75\t            BuildLighting();\r\n    76\t            BuildFloor();\r\n    77\t\r\n    78\t            var doorRoot = BuildDoor(out var door);\r\n    79\t            BuildWalls(doorRoot.transform.position);\r\n    80\t\r\n    81\t            BuildPlayer(out var movement, out var interactionController, out var health, out var debugControl,\r\n    82\t                out var mana, out var debugManaControl);\r\n    83\t            SetPrivateField(movement, \"interactionController\", interactionController);\r\n    84\t\r\n    85\t            // BuildCamera reads followTarget.position immediately (not as a live reference)\r\n    86\t            // to place the camera at its initial isometric framing, so BuildPlayer must run\r\n    87\t            // first. If this ordering is ever changed, BuildCamera's null-target warning below\r\n    88\t            // will fire rather than silently producing an unframed camera at the world origin.\r\n    89\t            BuildCamera(movement.transform);\r\n    90\t\r\n    91\t            BuildUI(door, debugControl, mana, debugManaControl);\r\n    92\t        }\r\n    93\t\r\n    94\t        private static void EnsureFolder(string path)\r\n    95\t        {\r\n    96\t            if (AssetDatabase.IsValidFolder(path)) return;\r\n    97\t\r\n    98\t            var parts = path.Split('/');\r\n    99\t            var current = parts[0];\r\n   100\t            for (var i = 1; i < parts.Length; i++)\r\n   101\t            {\r\n   102\t                var next = current + \"/\" + parts[i];\r\n   103\t                if (!AssetDatabase.IsValidFolder(next))\r\n   104\t                {\r\n   105\t                    AssetDatabase.CreateFolder(current, parts[i]);\r\n   106\t                }\r\n   107\t                current = next;\r\n   108\t            }\r\n   109\t        }\r\n   110\t\r\n     1\t# CURRENT STATE — No Safe Circle AI Pipeline\n     2\t\n     3\t> Update this file whenever a milestone or important implementation slice changes.\n     4\t\n     5\tLast updated: 2026-08-23, for Stage 4C OpenAI/Codex AgentRuntime and ArchitectureReview migration on `provider-adapters`.\n     6\t\n     7\t## Current Phase\n     8\t\n     9\t**Architecture Correction Phase 3 — COMPLETE AND MERGED.**\n    10\t\n    11\tPhase 3A — Evidence-Derived Current Conformance Evaluator — is complete.\n    12\t\n    13\tPhase 3B — First Real Production Baseline — is complete.\n    14\t\n    15\tPhase 3 was fast-forward merged into `main` at:\n    16\t\n    17\t```text\n    18\t43fdf0a163e281204906abd43a241db211587a0f\n    19\t```\n    20\t\n    21\t**Provider-Neutral Execution Crew Plan — Stage 4A: MAPPING, ENFORCEMENT, AND TRANSPORT-EVALUATION DECISIONS COMPLETE.**\n    22\t\n    23\tThe current development branch is:\n    24\t\n    25\t```text\n    26\tprovider-adapters\n    27\t```\n    28\t\n    29\tThe Stage 3 foundation now implements:\n    30\t\n    31\t- provider-neutral immutable `AgentInvocationRequest` and `AgentResult` contracts;\n    32\t- semantic capability and write-boundary contracts;\n    33\t- `low_cost`, `standard`, and `high_reasoning` model capability classes;\n    34\t- strict provider configuration loading and validation;\n    35\t- bounded request budgets and normalized failure classifications;\n    36\t- strict JSON value and supported-schema validation;\n    37\t- atomic, no-overwrite immutable run artifacts;\n    38\t- provider-neutral base interfaces;\n    39\t- a deterministic, side-effect-free `FakeProvider`;\n    40\t- an adversarial regression suite covering provider trust boundaries, path safety, schema failures, immutable artifacts, malformed provider values, and historical-directory protection.\n    41\t\n    42\tThe Stage 3 implementation is integrated; Stage 4A changes are documentation and decision work only.\n    43\t\n    44\tLive CLI discovery is complete. Non-production-write structured-output success probes completed for Claude Code and OpenAI/Codex. The provider-transport adopt-versus-build spike is also complete: `agent-mux` and `agent-shell` were evaluated and are not being adopted as production dependencies. ADR-035 was amended on 2026-08-23 to keep `AgentRequest` schema 1.0 and its required integer `turn_limit`, with explicit provider-specific enforcement mappings.\n    45\t\n    46\t**Stage 4B.2 — Practical Repository Read/Search** extends the existing Claude adapter for this trusted single-user local environment. `repository_read` maps to Claude `Read`; `repository_search` maps to Claude `Glob` and `Grep`; their combination exposes all three. Repository-capable calls use the actual source repository root (`/workspace` in Docker), while empty-capability calls retain their fresh empty temporary workspace and no-tool behavior. Validated `context_paths` are accepted only with repository capability and become prompt guidance rather than a hardened sandbox boundary.\n    47\t\n    48\t**Stage 4C — OpenAI/Codex Provider and ArchitectureReview Migration** adds `OpenAICodexProvider` behind generic AgentRuntime. The active OpenAI ArchitectureReview path now constructs task-neutral `AgentInvocationRequest` values and calls `AgentRunner`; provider subprocess, JSONL/final-output parsing, usage normalization, timeout translation, and failure normalization live in AgentRuntime. Invocation audit artifacts are nested under each ArchitectureReview run. TaskExecution is not involved.\n    49\t\n    50\tAgentRuntime `approved_command_execution`, repository writing, web access, non-null token limits, provider fallback, and production orchestration remain unsupported. The Codex ArchitectureReview repository read/search implementation uses Codex's ordinary read-only shell/file inspection inside the externally read-only `codex-review` Docker environment; that is not approved command execution. Stage 4C remains the OpenAI/Codex Provider stage.\n    51\t\n    52\tArchitectureReview generated runs and provider-latest views are separated under `outputs/claude/` and `outputs/codex/`; those provider-specific latest views retain convenience copies. Global `outputs/latest/` contains only one atomic `LATEST.json` pointer identifying the most recently completed provider-scoped run across providers. Manifests record the explicit provider namespace, resume cannot cross it, and failed/partial runs do not publish latest. Each provider retains eight independent reviewer roles. New independent reviewers ignore prior review opinions even when summarized in architecture/current-state documentation, while still using implemented architecture, accepted decisions, and current documented facts as evidence to judge independently. This preserves review provenance and supports a later Claude-versus-Codex comparison without implementing that comparison pipeline.\n    53\t\n    54\t**Provider-Neutral Execution Crew Plan — Stage 4: Implement Claude Code and OpenAI/Codex adapters against the same AgentRuntime fixtures.**\n    55\t\n    56\tThe accepted Stage 4 turn-budget mapping is:\n    57\t\n    58\t- Claude: `turn_limit=N` -> native `--max-turns N`, also bounded by external `timeout_seconds`;\n    59\t- OpenAI/Codex: no observed native turn limit, so use temporary `OPENAI_SECONDS_PER_TURN = 30` and `effective_timeout_seconds = min(timeout_seconds, turn_limit * 30)`;\n    60\t- these mappings are bounded-execution controls, not cross-provider-equivalent units of model work;\n    61\t- any non-null `token_limit` remains unsupported and fails closed.\n    62\t\n    63\tNo production `ExecutionCrew` role orchestration, Unity execution by an agent, GER integration, task selection, dependency readiness, autonomous dispatch, automatic Git commit/merge behavior, or live provider fallback has been enabled.\n    64\t\n    65\tArchitecture Correction Phase 3 remains intentionally fail-closed for execution:\n    66\t\n    67\t```text\n    68\tTASK READINESS: UNAVAILABLE — DISPATCH POLICY NOT ENABLED\n    69\t```\n    70\t\n    71\tand:\n    72\t\n    73\t```text\n    74\tEXECUTION AUTHORIZATION: DENIED\n    75\t```\n    76\t\n    77\tEvidence-derived conformance has been proven for `NSC-023`, but a conformant task does not establish dependency readiness or execution authority.\n    78\t\n    79\t## Architecture Review Result\n    80\t\n    81\tThe post-Milestone-1 architecture review evaluated one frozen repository commit through eight independent specialist reviews, a synthesis, and an adversarial critique of that synthesis.\n    82\t\n    83\tResult:\n    84\t\n    85\t- eight of eight independent reviewers: `partially_unsound`;\n    86\t- synthesis: `partially_unsound`;\n    87\t- adversarial critique: `synthesis_needs_revision`.\n    88\t\n    89\tAccepted correction direction:\n    90\t\n    91\t- preserve bounded workers, deterministic validation, GER repair, runtime evidence, human authority, and persistent task contracts;\n    92\t- stop treating mutable task metadata as completion truth;\n    93\t- distinguish historical delivery evidence from current conformance;\n    94\t- use reconciliation for bootstrap, broad audits, and reviewed change-impact proposals rather than routine global truth regeneration;\n    95\t- avoid implementing the entire original Milestone 2 bundle before proving smaller real delivery loops;\n    96\t- keep one worker and human merge/design authority until the one-ticket process is trustworthy;\n    97\t- do not treat reviewer consensus or task-graph ordering as automatic product priority.\n    98\t\n    99\tAccepted immutable review evidence is preserved under:\n   100\t\n   101\t`Pipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/`\n   102\t\n   103\tThat directory contains the frozen manifest, model assignments, all eight independent reviews, synthesis, and adversarial critique.\n   104\t\n   105\tGenerated `Pipeline/ArchitectureReview/outputs/` remains transient and ignored by default.\n   106\t\n   107\t## Architecture Correction Phase 1 — COMPLETE\n   108\t\n   109\tPhase 1 prevented the legacy task model from authorizing autonomous work before the task-contract migration and evidence model existed.\n   110\t\n   575\t## Source-of-Truth Boundaries\n   576\t\n   577\t### Game-design canon\n   578\t\n   579\tThe current human-approved GDD and explicit approved design decisions define intended behavior.\n   580\t\n   581\t### Production GDD retrieval\n   582\t\n   583\t`Pipeline/GDDRAG` provides current, hash-bound, integrity-validated retrieval over the canonical GDD. It is not a substitute for complete canon when retrieval coverage has not been proven.\n   584\t\n   585\t### Task contracts\n   586\t\n   587\t`Tasks/*.yaml` defines approved work identity, scope, dependencies, acceptance criteria, completion gates, downstream obligations, exclusive resources, contract revision/disposition, and provenance.\n   588\t\n   589\tTask contracts do not contain current execution or completion truth.\n   590\t\n   591\t### Integrated implementation\n   592\t\n   593\tThe integrated Git tree is the authority for what code and assets are present.\n   594\t\n   595\tPresence alone is not completion proof.\n   596\t\n   597\t### Delivery/revalidation records\n   598\t\n   599\tPhase 3 records are immutable evidence claims tied to exact task, canon, Git, gate, artifact, and approval identities.\n   600\t\n   601\tA record does not contain mutable current-completion authority.\n   602\t\n   603\t### Current conformance\n   604\t\n   605\t`Pipeline/TaskGraph/current_conformance.py` deterministically evaluates committed evidence against current committed HEAD.\n   606\t\n   607\tState inspection is now implemented.\n   608\t\n   609\tProduction proof now exists for NSC-023 through committed baseline `BASE-NSC-023-86af98f41ab5`. No real production revalidation record exists yet.\n   610\t\n   611\t### AgentRuntime requests/results\n   612\t\n   613\t`Pipeline/AgentRuntime` is the provider-neutral execution boundary for future model invocations.\n   614\t\n   615\tIts immutable request, result, configuration, and provider-log artifacts are execution records and audit data. They are **not** game-design authority, deterministic validation evidence, current conformance, readiness, dispatch authorization, or human approval.\n   616\t\n   617\tProvider-reported changed paths, executed commands, tests, usage, and structured output remain claims until independently checked by the appropriate deterministic Git, Unity, schema, TaskGraph, or human process.\n   618\t\n   619\t### Reconciliation\n   620\t\n   621\tReconciliation outputs remain immutable point-in-time observations. They may propose graph changes but do not directly mutate living task contracts or current conformance state.\n   622\t\n   623\tRoutine GDD iteration should eventually use reviewed, scoped impact analysis rather than global reconciliation after every edit.\n   624\t\n   625\t### Architecture review evidence\n   626\t\n   627\tThe preserved architecture review is primary evidence for the accepted correction direction, but reviewer recommendations do not automatically become project decisions.\n   628\t\n   629\t## Immediate Next Goal\n   630\t\n   631\t### Migrate ArchitectureReview through generic AgentRuntime using Claude\n   632\t\n   633\tStage 4B.2 repository read/search and Stage 4B.3 responsibility separation are complete. `AgentInvocationRequest` schema 1.0 is generic; `TaskExecutionRequest` schema 1.0 separately owns NSC identity; `AgentResult` remains schema 1.0. `ProviderOutputInvalid` normalization is already implemented.\n   634\t\n   635\tThe next useful production proof is to migrate ArchitectureReview through AgentRuntime using the existing Claude adapter. That later slice should retain ArchitectureReview workflow identity above AgentRuntime and should not invent a task identity. The OpenAI/Codex provider remains unimplemented.\n   636\t\n   637\tCurrent provider budget policy remains:\n   638\t\n   639\t- Claude Code: native `--max-turns N` plus external `timeout_seconds`;\n   640\t- OpenAI/Codex: `effective_timeout_seconds = min(timeout_seconds, turn_limit * 30)` using the temporary 30-seconds-per-turn-budget-unit policy;\n   641\t- non-null `token_limit`: fail closed;\n   642\t- no automatic model/provider fallback.\n   643\t\n   644\tStage 4 must remain bounded:\n   645\t\n   646\t- no provider-specific task contracts or role schemas;\n   647\t- no automatic provider fallback that changes task meaning;\n   648\t- no production `ExecutionCrew` role orchestration yet;\n   649\t- no real gameplay task execution yet;\n   650\t- no Unity validation delegated to a model;\n   651\t- no Phase 3 evidence publication by an adapter;\n   652\t- no dependency readiness;\n   653\t- no dispatch authorization;\n   654\t- no automatic merge.\n   655\t\n   656\tArchitecture Correction Phase 3 authority remains active:\n   657\t\n   658\t- `NSC-023` derives as `conformant`;\n   659\t- readiness remains unavailable;\n   660\t- authorization remains denied;\n   661\t- no worker, provider, runtime, or future adapter result establishes completion, readiness, or dispatch authority.\n   662\t\n   663\tNo real production revalidation record exists yet. Do not fabricate a gameplay, contract, GDD, scene, implementation, or test change merely to produce one. The first legitimate relevant change will exercise the production revalidation path.\n   664\t\n   665\t## Real Gameplay Task Selection After the Evidence Proof\n   666\t\n   667\tThe first gameplay implementation task is **not automatically selected** by this document, the graph, or reviewer consensus.\n   668\t\n   669\t`NSC-037` was discussed during architecture review as a possible delivery-lane experiment. It is not the committed gameplay priority.\n   670\t\n   671\t`NSC-003` remains a possible high-leverage gameplay anchor, but it should not be handed to a worker unchanged merely because its task contract currently says `single_agent`.\n   672\t\n   673\tBefore implementing NSC-003, human review should resolve:\n   674\t\n   675\t- which Input Action owns click-to-move/select;\n   676\t- arbitration among UI click, movement, Fireball, Frost Field, Force Wave, and door selection;\n   677\t- click versus held-pointer steering;\n   678\t- destination replacement and cancellation;\n   679\t- approach/arrival semantics;\n   680\t- movement pathing expectations;\n   681\t- movement-restriction ownership/release semantics.\n   682\t\n   683\tHuman review should also decide whether NSC-003 should be superseded by smaller contracts, for example:\n   684\t\n   685\t- runtime input and shared pointer-projection foundation;\n   686\t- mouse-directed movement, restriction, reset, and suspension.\n   687\t\n   688\t## Deferred Until Evidence Justifies It\n   689\t\n   690\tDo not enable or build these merely because Phase 3 exists:\n   691\t\n   692\t- dependency-derived `taskcontrol ready`;\n   693\t- autonomous execution authorization;\n   694\t- autonomous task claiming or continuous dispatch;\n   695\t- parallel workers or broad worktree orchestration;\n   696\t- automatic merge/merge queues;\n   697\t- broad GitHub Issues/Projects synchronization;\n   698\t- automatic backlog replenishment;\n   699\t- full-game speculative decomposition;\n   700\t- Progressive Decomposer as a bundled platform;\n   701\t- Artifact Authority without a real blocking design need;\n   702\t- top-k-only task context presented as complete canon;\n   703\t- automatic GDD impact analysis without a reviewed causal model;\n   704\t- a general supervisor before repeated delivery bottlenecks justify one.\n   705\t\n   706\tThese remain later candidates driven by observed production needs rather than roadmap momentum.\n   707\t\n   708\t## Obsolete Branch/Plan Warning\n   709\t\n   710\tDo not resume `milestone-2a-nsc-003-context` unchanged.\n   711\t\n   712\tThat branch predates the accepted architecture corrections, task-contract schema 2.0, reviewed migration corrections, production GDD RAG integrity boundary, and Phase 3 conformance model.\n   713\t\n   714\tUseful ideas should be recreated from the corrected current repository state rather than continuing the obsolete plan as written.\n   715\t\n   716\t## Next Window Instructions\n   717\t\n   718\tRead, in order:\n   719\t\n   720\t1. `Docs/AI-Pipeline/START_HERE.md`;\n   721\t2. this file;\n   722\t3. `Docs/AI-Pipeline/00_MASTER_CONTEXT.md`;\n   723\t4. `Docs/AI-Pipeline/DECISIONS.md`;\n   724\t5. `Docs/AI-Pipeline/06_PROVIDER_NEUTRAL_EXECUTION_CREW_PLAN.md`;\n   725\t6. `Docs/AI-Pipeline/ADR-034_PROVIDER_NEUTRAL_AGENT_RUNTIME.md`;\n   726\t7. `Docs/AI-Pipeline/07_PROVIDER_ADAPTER_CAPABILITY_MAPPING.md`;\n   727\t8. `Docs/AI-Pipeline/ADR-035_PROVIDER_ADAPTER_ENFORCEMENT.md`;\n   728\t9. `Pipeline/AgentRuntime/README.md`;\n   729\t10. `Pipeline/AgentRuntime/contracts.py`;\n   730\t11. `Pipeline/AgentRuntime/agent_runner.py`;\n   731\t12. `Pipeline/AgentRuntime/providers/base.py`;\n   732\t13. `Pipeline/AgentRuntime/tests/agent_runtime_smoke_test.py`;\n   733\t14. inspect the actual repository and branch state.\n   734\t\n   735\tThen:\n     1\t# START HERE — No Safe Circle AI Pipeline\n     2\t\n     3\tThis is the first file any AI assistant or developer should read before working on the autonomous development pipeline.\n     4\t\n     5\t## Purpose\n     6\t\n     7\tThe pipeline is built across multiple work sessions and AI contexts. Do not rely on conversation memory as the source of truth.\n     8\t\n     9\t**The repository is the source of truth.**\n    10\t\n    11\t## Current Status Snapshot\n    12\t\n    13\tStage 4B.2 practical repository read/search and Stage 4B.3 AgentRuntime/TaskExecution separation remain complete on `provider-adapters`. The current implementation slice is **Stage 4C — OpenAI/Codex AgentRuntime and ArchitectureReview migration**. The initial fail-closed `ClaudeCodeProvider` and shared bounded `StandardProcessRunner` were committed at:\n    14\t\n    15\t```text\n    16\tae046fd828f168dac6c87c49878fe1812f6c1fd7\n    17\t```\n    18\t\n    19\tThe provider preserves structured-output invocations with empty capabilities and now also supports:\n    20\t\n    21\t```text\n    22\trepository_read -> Claude Read\n    23\trepository_search -> Claude Glob and Grep\n    24\tboth -> Claude Read, Glob, and Grep\n    25\ttoken_limit = null\n    26\t```\n    27\t\n    28\tEmpty-capability invocations still run in a fresh empty temporary workspace with no tools. Repository-capable Claude invocations run in the actual repository root (`/workspace` in Docker) and may accept validated repository-relative `context_paths` as prompt guidance. Repository writing, AgentRuntime `approved_command_execution`, and web access remain unsupported. Codex ArchitectureReview read/search uses ordinary read-only shell/file inspection inside the externally read-only `codex-review` Docker environment; that is not approved command execution. See ADR-038 for the trusted single-user boundary and accepted limited read-containment risk.\n    29\t\n    30\tThe deterministic AgentRuntime, process-runner, and Claude-provider suites all pass. Before Stage 4C work, the opt-in live Claude repository read/search smoke also passed through `AgentRunner`. This proves the Claude adapter path; it does not mean the direct Claude ArchitectureReview runner has migrated to AgentRuntime.\n    31\t\n    32\tStage 4C implements OpenAI/Codex behind generic AgentRuntime and migrates ArchitectureReview as the first real existing non-task consumer. Codex supports empty capability, repository read, repository search, and their combination; repository access requires an explicit externally enforced read-only profile and ArchitectureReview relies on the `codex-review` Docker mount. The approved timeout translation is:\n    33\t\n    34\t```text\n    35\teffective_timeout_seconds = min(timeout_seconds, turn_limit * 30)\n    36\t```\n    37\t\n    38\tThis is a bounded-execution policy, not a cross-provider-equivalent turn measurement.\n    39\t\n    40\tAgentRuntime exposes generic `AgentInvocationRequest` with no dependency on NSC task IDs or `Tasks/*.yaml`; TaskExecution separately owns NSC task identity. ArchitectureReview now reaches Codex through `AgentRunner` without TaskExecution. ExecutionCrew, Reconciliation migration, provider fallback, and write/command authority remain out of scope.\n    41\t\n    42\tArchitectureReview generated output is provider-scoped under `outputs/claude/` and `outputs/codex/`; each provider-specific latest view includes convenience copies of its synthesis and critique. Global `outputs/latest/` contains only one atomic `LATEST.json` pointer to the most recently completed provider-scoped run. Independent reviewers ignore prior review opinions even when architecture/current-state documents summarize them, while still evaluating implemented architecture, accepted decisions, and current facts. Both provider paths retain eight independent reviewer roles. The separation preserves provenance and enables later Claude-versus-Codex comparison; no comparison pipeline exists yet.\n    43\t\n    44\tAs of 2026-08-21:\n    45\t\n    46\t- **Milestone 1 — Persistent Work Graph is COMPLETE.**\n    47\t- The approved persistent graph contains 37 work records under `Tasks/`.\n    48\t- `taskcontrol validate`, `list`, `show`, `ready`, and `graph` work against the real persisted graph.\n    49\t- The first deterministic ready frontier contains seven executable `single_agent` implementation tasks.\n    50\t- Reconciliation/verification history is immutable; `Tasks/*.yaml` is the living operational work graph.\n    51\t- The one-time bootstrap is complete and must not be rerun.\n    52\t- Current `main` has been merged into `adversarial-architecture-review` so the architecture-review branch includes the completed Milestone 1 implementation.\n    53\t\n    54\tThe next planned infrastructure milestone is **Milestone 2 — RAG + Project Scanner + Compact Context + Progressive Decomposition + Artifact Authority**.\n    55\t\n    56\tBefore a large Milestone 2 investment, use the adversarial architecture review as a checkpoint against the now-M1-complete repository:\n    57\t\n    58\t`Pipeline/ArchitectureReview/README.md`\n    59\t\n    60\tThe next infrastructure work should also advance a real No Safe Circle task rather than creating another infrastructure-only stretch.\n    61\t\n    62\t## Required Reading Order\n    63\t\n    64\t1. Read `Docs/AI-Pipeline/CURRENT_STATE.md`.\n    65\t2. Read `Docs/AI-Pipeline/08_STAGE_4B_CLAUDE_CODE_PROVIDER.md` for the latest provider implementation state.\n    66\t3. Read `Docs/AI-Pipeline/ADR-037_CLAUDE_CODE_PROVIDER_BOUNDARY.md` and `Docs/AI-Pipeline/ADR-038_PRACTICAL_REPOSITORY_READ_SEARCH.md` for the Claude boundaries.\n    67\t4. Read `Docs/AI-Pipeline/00_MASTER_CONTEXT.md` for the target architecture.\n    68\t5. Read the milestone/context file named by `CURRENT_STATE.md`.\n    69\t6. Read `Docs/AI-Pipeline/DECISIONS.md` when the work touches architecture, Git workflow, task semantics, autonomy, RAG, GER, evaluation, refinement, validation, progressive decomposition, or artifact authority.\n    70\t7. Inspect the actual repository state before changing anything.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
